// generated from lessons/branch-*.json — do not edit by hand
const LESSON_BANK = {"1.1": {"title": "What Claude Code is", "intro": "Claude Code is easiest to define by what it is not: it is not a chatbot you paste code into, and not an autocomplete plugin. It is a CLI agent — a program that puts a Claude model in a loop with tools and lets it act on your machine.", "sections": [{"heading": "A CLI agent, not a chatbot", "body": "A chatbot produces text; you do the acting. You paste code in, read the answer, copy it back out, run it yourself. Claude Code inverts that: you describe an outcome — \"fix the failing tests\" — and it acts directly. It searches your project, reads the relevant files, edits them, runs the test suite, reads the failures, and edits again.\n\nIt runs in your terminal, launched with `claude` inside a project directory. From there it has what a chatbot never has: your files, your shell, your git state, and any command you could run yourself. That access is the whole difference. The model behind claude.ai and the model behind Claude Code can be the same weights — what changes is that Claude Code wraps the model in machinery that can execute what the model decides."}, {"heading": "Not an autocomplete plugin either", "body": "The other common confusion is with inline IDE assistants. Autocomplete-style tools see the file you have open and predict the next few lines — fast, local, single-file. Claude Code sees the whole project and works across it: coordinated edits in several files, running builds, tracing a bug from a stack trace through three modules, committing the result.\n\nClaude Code does ship IDE extensions (VS Code, JetBrains), plus a desktop app and a web surface — but these are front-ends to the same underlying engine, not a different product. The same `CLAUDE.md`, settings, and configuration apply everywhere. The mental model to keep: the product is the agent engine; terminal, IDE, and browser are just windows onto it."}, {"heading": "What \"agentic\" means, precisely", "body": "\"Agentic\" is not marketing for \"smart.\" It names a specific mechanism: the model runs in a loop with tools. Each cycle, the model can either reply with text or request an action — read this file, run this command. The harness executes the request, feeds the result back to the model, and the model decides its next step based on what it just learned. Anthropic describes the phases as gather context, take action, verify results.\n\nThe crucial property is the exit condition: the loop ends when the model stops requesting actions — when it judges the task done — not after a fixed number of steps and not when you press a key. That is why one prompt can trigger dozens of tool uses, and why Claude Code can course-correct mid-task: a failed test is just more information fed back into the loop."}, {"heading": "The division of labor", "body": "Anthropic's own definition is worth memorizing: Claude Code is \"the agentic harness around Claude.\" The model does exactly one thing — it reads a sequence of messages and produces tokens. It cannot touch a file, run a command, or remember anything on its own. Everything else — executing tools, managing permissions, reading `CLAUDE.md`, saving transcripts, assembling each request — is the harness.\n\nThis split is the key to every deeper topic. When Claude Code seems to \"remember\" your session, that is the harness re-sending history to a stateless model. When it \"takes an action,\" that is the harness executing a structured request the model wrote. Nothing about the agent is magic; it is a text-completion function plus a loop plus tools. Keep the model and the harness separate in your head and the rest of the machinery becomes legible."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude Code docs — Overview", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/overview"}, {"label": "Claude Code docs — How Claude Code works", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/how-claude-code-works"}]}, "1.2": {"title": "The four layers", "intro": "Everything you type travels down a four-layer stack — CLI → harness → API → model — and every response travels back up. Knowing which layer owns a behavior tells you where to look when something surprises you, and it is the map on which the Bedrock-vs-Anthropic question has a precise answer.", "sections": [{"heading": "The stack at a glance", "body": "The CLI is what you see: the terminal interface. The harness is the machinery underneath: it assembles requests, executes tools, enforces permissions. The API layer is the transport: a stateless HTTP service that accepts a message array and returns generated content. The model is the weights: a function from messages to tokens, running on the provider's hardware.\n\nThe first two layers live entirely on your machine. The last two live on the provider's side. That boundary — between harness and API — is the most important line in the whole system: everything above it is identical no matter how you connect, and everything below it is where providers differ. When you debug, your first question should be \"which layer does this behavior belong to?\""}, {"heading": "The CLI: the surface", "body": "The CLI layer is the interaction surface: rendering the conversation, reading your keystrokes, slash commands like `/clear` and `/model`, keyboard shortcuts, mode cycling with Shift+Tab, the permission prompts you approve or reject. It is purely local and purely presentational — no tokens are spent here, and nothing at this layer ever reaches the model directly.\n\nThat last point matters more than it seems. A slash command like `/compact` is not a message to Claude; it is an instruction to the harness, which then decides what (if anything) changes in future requests. Understanding that the CLI talks to the harness, not the model, explains why some commands take effect instantly and locally while your actual prompts cost tokens and take a round-trip."}, {"heading": "The harness: the machinery", "body": "The harness is the layer Anthropic names when it defines the product: \"the agentic harness around Claude.\" Its central job, performed on every single model call, is assembling the request — system prompt, tool definitions, `CLAUDE.md`, the full conversation so far, your new message. Its second job is executing what comes back: when the model requests a tool, the harness runs it locally, checks it against your permission rules, and feeds the result into the next request.\n\nThe harness also owns the local files (`~/.claude/`, transcripts, settings), context management and compaction, hooks, skills, and subagents. Every one of those features is provider-neutral: the harness behaves identically whether it is about to call Anthropic directly or AWS Bedrock, because its work happens before and after the API call, never inside it."}, {"heading": "The API layer: the swappable door", "body": "The API layer is the only layer that changes when you switch providers. It is a stateless transport: it accepts a complete message array, runs the model over it, streams back content, and forgets you. Anthropic direct, AWS Bedrock, and Google Vertex AI are three doors into essentially the same request/response shape.\n\nWhat differs at this layer: authentication (an Anthropic API key or OAuth login versus AWS credentials and IAM roles, enabled with `CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK=1`), model identifiers (Anthropic model names versus Bedrock model IDs and inference profiles), regions, quotas and throttling behavior, and billing. When you hit a rate limit, see a model-not-available error, or wonder why caching behaves differently — that is this layer, and the answer is provider-specific. Nothing above this layer knows or cares which door was used."}, {"heading": "The model: the same weights everywhere", "body": "At the bottom sits the model itself: Claude's weights, loaded on provider hardware. It is a pure function — message array in, tokens out. It holds no session, executes no tools, and reads no files; every apparent capability beyond generating text belongs to a layer above it.\n\nThe anti-confusion fact for Bedrock users: it is the same model. Bedrock does not host a modified Claude; the weights answering your request through AWS are the ones Anthropic trained. Differences you observe across providers — availability lag for new models, caching support, quota behavior — are API-layer differences, never model-layer ones.\n\nA quick diagnostic habit: \"it ignored my CLAUDE.md\" → harness. \"Throttling errors at 9am\" → API layer. \"The answer quality changed\" → model choice. \"My shortcut doesn't work\" → CLI. Four layers, four very different investigations."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude Code docs — How Claude Code works", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/how-claude-code-works"}, {"label": "Claude Code docs — Third-party integrations (Bedrock, Vertex)", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/third-party-integrations"}]}, "1.3": {"title": "The stateless loop", "intro": "This is the keystone fact of the whole system: the API stores nothing between calls. Every other topic — compaction, caching, cost, \"it forgot\" — is a consequence of it. Get this one right and the rest of the curriculum gets cheap.", "sections": [{"heading": "Nothing lives on the server", "body": "The Claude API is stateless. There is no session object on the server, no conversation ID, no memory of you between calls. Each request is a complete, self-contained array of messages, and the model reads it start to finish as if for the first time — because it is the first time. The same is true on Bedrock: statelessness is a property of the protocol, not of any one provider.\n\nThis feels wrong because your session so obviously \"remembers.\" It refers back to what you said ten minutes ago; it knows which files it already read. But nothing was remembered anywhere. The explanation is one sentence long, and it is the sentence to carry into every later topic: the harness re-sent it. All of it. Again."}, {"heading": "Memory is a resend", "body": "On every single model call, the harness rebuilds the entire request from scratch: the system prompt, the tool definitions, your `CLAUDE.md`, and the entire prior transcript — every message, every tool result — with your newest input appended at the end. Then it ships the whole array. Nothing is referenced or implied; everything the model \"knows\" is literally present in that one request.\n\nThis is the harness's central job, and it happens far more often than once per prompt: a single turn in which the model uses tools involves many model calls, each one a full resend of everything so far plus the latest tool result. The conversation the model sees is not stored server-side and retrieved — it is reconstructed and retransmitted, call after call, growing every time."}, {"heading": "The proof is on your disk", "body": "You do not have to take this on faith. Every model call is logged to `~/.claude/projects/<slug>/<sessionId>.jsonl`, including the API's own token accounting in a `usage` object. Two patterns in those numbers prove the resend.\n\nFirst, mid-session, genuinely new input (`input_tokens`) is often just a couple of tokens per call — everything else in the request had been sent before and hits the cache. Second, the tell: `cache_read_input_tokens` on call n almost exactly equals the total input of call n−1. Each call re-ships the previous call's entire payload and then some.\n\nThe totals are startling. One real session: 161 model calls, 29.4 million input tokens against 151 thousand output — a 195:1 ratio. Nobody typed 29 million tokens. One growing conversation was re-sent 161 times."}, {"heading": "Why it forgets nothing and remembers nothing", "body": "Both halves of the paradox resolve at once. The model remembers nothing: each call is first contact, and any sense of continuity is manufactured by the harness's resend. And the model forgets nothing: as long as a piece of text sits in the resent array, it is fully present — not fading, not \"far back in memory,\" but literally on the page the model is reading right now.\n\nSo when a session genuinely does \"forget\" something, the model is never the culprit. Either the harness dropped or summarized that content out of the array — which is exactly what compaction does when the context window fills — or it was never in the array to begin with. \"It forgot\" is always a statement about what the harness assembled, never about the model's mind."}, {"heading": "The consequences: quadratic cost, and why caching exists", "body": "Once you see the resend, the economics follow. A long session is not expensive because it is long — it is expensive because every turn pays for the whole conversation again. Turn n resends turns 1 through n−1, so session cost grows roughly quadratically with length. That, not tidiness, is why compaction must exist: it is the only thing standing between you and a quadratic bill.\n\nPrompt caching is what makes the model survivable at all. In that real session, 96% of all input tokens were cache reads, billed at roughly a tenth of list price. But be precise about what a cache read means: the tokens were still sent over the wire and still counted — the server matched them against a cached prefix and skipped recomputing them. Caching makes the resend affordable. It does not make it stop happening."}], "resources": [{"label": "Agent SDK docs — The agent loop", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/agent-loop"}, {"label": "Claude Code docs — Context window (with simulation)", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/context-window"}]}, "1.4": {"title": "One turn, end to end", "intro": "Follow one keystroke through the whole machine — assembly, send, reply, tool execution, resend — and every other topic in this quiz acquires a fixed address on the path. This is the map the rest of the branches hang from.", "sections": [{"heading": "Step 1 — Assemble: your sentence is not what gets sent", "body": "You type a sentence and press enter. The harness does not send your sentence. It builds a complete request around it: the system prompt (about 4,200 tokens, per Anthropic's context-window docs), the tool definitions, your `CLAUDE.md`, environment info, the entire prior transcript — and, last of all, your new message.\n\nThe ordering is not stylistic; it is load-bearing. The most stable content goes first and the most volatile last, because prompt caching matches a prefix from the start of the request. Keep the front of the request byte-identical across calls and it bills at the cheap cache-read rate; change something early and everything after it recomputes at full price. Prompt assembly, `CLAUDE.md`, skills, and the stability gradient all live here, at this step, inside the harness."}, {"heading": "Step 2 — Send and reply: content blocks", "body": "The harness ships the whole array to the API. Nothing is referenced or implied — every token the model will consider is literally in this request, because the API holds no state from the last one.\n\nThe model replies not with a plain string but with a sequence of typed content blocks: `thinking` (reasoning, billed as output), `text` (prose for you), and `tool_use`. A `tool_use` block is the pivotal one — it carries an `id`, a tool `name`, and structured `input`, and it is a request, not an action. The model cannot read a file or run a command; it can only emit a structured description of what it wants done. Whether anything actually happens is entirely up to the harness — which is exactly where permissions get their leverage."}, {"heading": "Steps 3–4 — Execute and resend: the inner loop", "body": "If the reply contains a `tool_use` block, the harness executes it locally — reads the file, runs the command — checks it against your permission rules, and appends the output to the conversation as a `tool_result` block. Then it goes back to step 2: same request, now longer. And again, and again.\n\nThis inner loop has no human in it, and it exits on one condition only: the model returns a reply containing no `tool_use` block. Not a step count, not you. That is why \"one turn\" — one press of enter — can be dozens of model calls; 161 in one observed session. Each iteration is a full resend of everything so far, which is where the quadratic cost curve comes from and why a tool that returns a huge output makes every subsequent call in the turn more expensive."}, {"heading": "The detail worth a whiteboard: tool results wear your name", "body": "The `tool_result` block goes back into the conversation as a `user` message. The model asked for a file; the answer arrives on the same channel your typed words do. From inside the message array, file contents, command output, and human speech are indistinguishable in kind — one input channel, shared by everything.\n\nThis is why prompt injection via tool results is a protocol-level security category rather than a bug to be patched. If a fetched web page or a file in your repo contains text shaped like instructions, it enters the model's input the same way your instructions do. The defense cannot be \"the model ignores that channel\" — there is no separate channel. It has to come from the harness: permissions, sandboxing, and your own review of what the loop is allowed to touch."}, {"heading": "Where everything sits — and where Bedrock differs", "body": "Every quiz branch has an address on this path. Assembly (step 1): prompt layering, `CLAUDE.md`, skills injection, the stability gradient, compaction — all harness, all provider-neutral. The wire (step 2): auth, model IDs, caching economics, rate limits — the API layer. Inside the model call: prefill, decode, sampling, the context window. Execution (step 3): permissions, hooks, MCP, sandboxing — harness again.\n\nThe Bedrock delta is now precise: only the send step changes. On Bedrock the request is authenticated with AWS credentials instead of an Anthropic key, addressed to an AWS endpoint with a Bedrock model ID, and subject to AWS quotas and billing. Everything before the wire and everything after it — assembly, the loop, tool execution, the transcript on disk — is byte-for-byte the same machinery. Bedrock is a different door, not a different building."}], "resources": [{"label": "Agent SDK docs — The agent loop", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/agent-loop"}, {"label": "Claude Code docs — Context window", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/context-window"}, {"label": "Claude API docs — Prompt caching", "url": "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching"}]}, "1.5": {"title": "Usage patterns", "intro": "Claude Code is one engine with several distinct ways of driving it. Choosing the right pattern — interactive, plan-first, or headless — is mostly about how much you need to steer, and how much you already trust the task.", "sections": [{"heading": "Interactive sessions: the default", "body": "Run `claude` in a project directory and you get the interactive REPL: a running conversation in which the agent works, asks permission when it needs it, and you steer. You can interrupt at any point — Escape stops the current action; typing a correction mid-turn queues it for the model to read as soon as the current tool call finishes. You iterate rather than restart: \"that's not quite right, the issue is in session handling\" is a normal move, not a failure.\n\nSessions are saved locally as transcripts, so work can span sittings: `claude --continue` resumes the most recent session in the directory, `claude --resume` lets you pick one. Interactive mode is the right default whenever the task is exploratory, ambiguous, or one where you expect to course-correct."}, {"heading": "Headless and CI: claude -p", "body": "The `-p` (print) flag turns Claude Code into a one-shot Unix tool: it takes a prompt, runs the full agentic loop non-interactively, prints the result to stdout, and exits. It composes with pipes — `git diff main --name-only | claude -p \"review these files for security issues\"` — which makes it the building block for CI jobs, pre-commit hooks, and batch processing.\n\nBecause there is no human to answer permission prompts, headless runs need permissions decided in advance, via settings files or flags — otherwise the run blocks or the action is refused. Output format flags let you get structured JSON instead of prose for scripting.\n\nThis is where the Bedrock setup quietly shines: auth comes from environment variables and IAM roles (`CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK=1` plus AWS credentials), with no interactive `/login` step — exactly what a CI runner wants."}, {"heading": "Plan mode: separate thinking from doing", "body": "Plan mode restricts the agent to reading and analysis: it explores the codebase and proposes a plan, but edits nothing until you approve. Enter it with `claude --permission-mode plan`, or cycle modes mid-session with Shift+Tab (default → accept-edits → plan).\n\nThe workflow it enables is two-phase: research first — \"read `src/auth/` and understand how we handle sessions, then plan the OAuth support\" — then review and refine the plan in conversation, then approve and let the agent implement. For complex or risky changes this reliably beats jumping straight to code, because you catch a wrong approach when it costs a paragraph, not a diff across nine files.\n\nMechanically, plan mode is a permission mode — a harness-level constraint on which tools may act — not a different model or a smarter setting. The same loop runs; it is simply not allowed to write."}, {"heading": "Claude Code vs claude.ai", "body": "The dividing line is access. A claude.ai chat has only what you paste into it; you carry code in, carry answers out, and execute everything yourself. Claude Code has your repository, your shell, and your git state, and closes the loop itself — edit, run, verify. Rule of thumb: for a question, chat is fine; for a change to a codebase, use the agent.\n\nThe surfaces blur at the edges — claude.ai/code runs Claude Code sessions on cloud VMs, and IDE extensions wrap the same engine — but the question \"does the assistant need to act on my files?\" still sorts almost every case.\n\nOne practical delta for Bedrock users: claude.ai is a subscription product tied to an Anthropic account, while your Claude Code usage bills through AWS as API traffic. The interfaces differ in governance and billing, not in the model behind them."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude Code docs — Quickstart", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/quickstart"}, {"label": "Claude Code docs — Common workflows", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/common-workflows"}, {"label": "Claude Code docs — Non-interactive (headless) mode", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/headless"}]}, "2.1": {"title": "Slash commands", "intro": "Slash commands are instructions you type into the same prompt box as normal conversation, but most of them talk to the harness, not the model. Knowing which command touches which layer is the whole trick.", "sections": [{"heading": "What a slash command actually is", "body": "Anything starting with `/` is intercepted by the harness before prompt assembly. Typing `/` opens a menu of everything invocable: built-in commands, bundled and user-authored skills, and commands contributed by plugins or MCP servers. The key mechanical split: most built-in commands execute fixed logic locally in the CLI process — `/config` opens a settings UI, `/clear` resets the message array — and never produce an API call at all. Skill-style commands are the opposite: they expand into prompt text that gets injected into the next request, so the model does the work.\n\nThis matters because of the stateless loop: the harness rebuilds and resends the whole conversation every turn. A command that mutates harness state (the selected model, the context contents, permission rules) changes what is assembled and sent on every turn that follows."}, {"heading": "Session hygiene: /clear, /compact, /resume, /rewind", "body": "`/clear` starts a fresh conversation: the message array the harness resends drops to empty, so your next turn is cheap again. The old session isn't destroyed — its transcript stays on disk and you can return to it with `/resume`. `/compact` keeps the session but replaces the accumulated history with a model-written summary, freeing context at the cost of one full read of the conversation; you can pass instructions, like `/compact keep the API design decisions`. `/rewind` (also double-`Esc` on an empty prompt) rolls code and conversation back to a checkpoint.\n\nRule of thumb: switching tasks entirely → `/clear`; same task, context filling up → `/compact`; Claude went down a bad path → `/rewind`."}, {"heading": "Inspection: /help, /status, /context, /usage, /model", "body": "`/help` lists available commands. `/status` reports version, working directory, active model, and — crucially for Bedrock users — which provider and region the session resolved, and where the region came from. `/context` visualizes how the context window is being spent per category (system prompt, tools, skills, messages), which is the fastest way to see why a session feels heavy. `/usage` (alias `/cost`) shows token consumption. `/model` switches models mid-session; on Bedrock the choices resolve through Bedrock model IDs or inference profiles rather than bare Anthropic model names, and only models your AWS account can invoke will actually work."}, {"heading": "Setup and configuration commands", "body": "`/init` explores the project and generates a `CLAUDE.md` so future sessions start with codebase knowledge baked in. `/config` (alias `/settings`) opens the settings interface. `/memory` edits your `CLAUDE.md` files directly. `/permissions` manages allow/deny rules, `/mcp` manages MCP server connections (including per-server reconnect and enable/disable), `/agents` manages subagent definitions, and `/hooks` shows hook configuration. All of these ultimately edit the same on-disk files (`settings.json` tiers, `CLAUDE.md`, `.mcp.json`) that you could edit by hand — the commands are just guided front-ends over them."}, {"heading": "/login and the Bedrock delta", "body": "`/login` and `/logout` manage sign-in to an Anthropic account (Claude subscription or Console API key) — and they are exactly the commands a Bedrock user never runs. With `CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK=1` set, authentication comes from the standard AWS SDK credential chain: environment variables, an `AWS_PROFILE`, SSO, IAM roles, or a Bedrock API key via `AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK`. `/logout` is unavailable on Bedrock because there is no harness-held credential to discard — you \"log out\" by removing AWS credentials.\n\nIf a colleague's setup guide says \"run /login\", that's the tell they're on the Anthropic API. On Bedrock, `/status` is your equivalent sanity check: it shows the provider, the resolved region, and the models in play. Every other command in this lesson behaves identically on both providers."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude Code docs — Commands reference", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/commands"}, {"label": "Claude Code docs — Claude Code on Amazon Bedrock", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/amazon-bedrock"}]}, "2.2": {"title": "Modes: default, plan, auto-accept & permission modes", "intro": "Permission modes set the baseline for what Claude may do without stopping to ask you. The model behaves the same in every mode — it always emits tool calls; the mode decides whether the harness executes them immediately or pauses for approval.", "sections": [{"heading": "Why modes exist, and where they live", "body": "In the stateless loop, the model's output is just structured text: a `tool_use` block saying \"run this command\" or \"edit this file\". Nothing executes until the harness executes it. Permission modes are a harness-side policy on that execution step — a dial for how often the loop pauses for a human. Nothing about the mode is sent to the model as a restriction (plan mode partially excepted, since the harness also tells the model to research rather than act), which is why modes are enforced reliably: they're code, not instructions the model might ignore.\n\nThe tradeoff is oversight versus flow. Tight modes make you review each action as it comes; loose modes let Claude work in long uninterrupted stretches and report back."}, {"heading": "The mode roster", "body": "`default` (labeled Manual in the UI): only reads run without asking; every edit and non-read-only command prompts. `acceptEdits`: file edits plus common filesystem commands (`mkdir`, `touch`, `mv`, `cp`, `rm`, `sed`) auto-approve, but only for paths inside the working directory or added directories — everything else still prompts. `plan`: read-and-research only; edits are blocked until you approve a plan. `bypassPermissions`: everything runs without asking — intended only for isolated containers and VMs. Two less common modes: `dontAsk` runs only pre-approved tools (locked-down CI), and `auto` lets everything run but has a separate classifier model review each action in the background, blocking things like `curl | bash`, force pushes, or credential exfiltration. Auto mode availability depends on plan, model, and provider — it works on Bedrock, but only with newer models."}, {"heading": "Plan mode in practice", "body": "Plan mode is the \"look before you leap\" workflow: Claude reads files, runs exploratory commands, and writes up a proposed approach, but does not touch your source. When the plan is ready it's presented with approval options — approve and auto-accept the resulting edits, approve but review each edit manually, or keep planning. Approving exits plan mode and switches the session into the mode the chosen option describes.\n\nThe reason this workflow earns its keep: an agent that starts editing on a misunderstanding wastes both your time and a lot of resent tokens. A plan is a cheap checkpoint on direction. Enter it by cycling with `Shift+Tab`, prefixing a single prompt with `/plan`, or starting the session with `claude --permission-mode plan`."}, {"heading": "Modes versus permission rules", "body": "Modes set the baseline; allow/deny rules from `settings.json` or `/permissions` layer on top, and the two compose predictably. Deny rules and explicit ask rules apply in every mode — including `bypassPermissions` — so an org can hard-block `Bash(rm -rf *)` regardless of how permissive the session is. Allow rules pre-approve specific tools in the stricter modes but are meaningless in bypass, where everything is already approved. A separate invariant: writes to protected paths (repository internals, Claude's own configuration) are never auto-approved outside `bypassPermissions`, no matter the mode. So the mental model is: mode = default answer to \"may I?\", rules = specific exceptions that always win."}, {"heading": "Switching modes and setting defaults", "body": "Mid-session, `Shift+Tab` cycles `default` → `acceptEdits` → `plan`, plus any optional modes you've enabled (`bypassPermissions` only appears in the cycle if the session started with it allowed, e.g. via `--allow-dangerously-skip-permissions`). The status bar always shows the active mode, like `⏸ plan mode on` or `⏵⏵ accept edits on`. At startup, pass `--permission-mode <mode>`; the same flag works with `-p` for non-interactive runs. As a persistent default, set `permissions.defaultMode` in a settings file — `~/.claude/settings.json` for yourself, `.claude/settings.json` to make plan mode the default for a whole project. Modes are entirely provider-neutral: this machinery is identical on Bedrock and the Anthropic API."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude Code docs — Permission modes", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/permission-modes"}, {"label": "Claude Code docs — Permissions", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/permissions"}]}, "2.3": {"title": "Keyboard shortcuts", "intro": "A handful of keystrokes cover most of the daily interaction: stopping Claude, recalling history, entering multiline prompts, and the prefix characters that change what the input line means.", "sections": [{"heading": "Stopping, steering, exiting", "body": "`Esc` is the interrupt: it stops the current response or tool call mid-turn so you can redirect, and Claude keeps the work done so far — because each turn is a fresh request, interrupting just means the harness stops the current API call and waits for your next input. `Ctrl+C` interrupts a running operation too; on an idle prompt the first press clears your input and a second press exits Claude Code. `Ctrl+D` exits (press twice to confirm). Double-`Esc` is context-dependent: with a draft in the input it clears the draft (recoverable with `Up`); on an empty input it opens the rewind menu to restore code and conversation from an earlier checkpoint."}, {"heading": "History and search", "body": "`Up`/`Down` arrows navigate your prompt history, which is stored per working directory. In a multi-row input the arrows first move the cursor within the prompt; once at the top or bottom row, pressing again steps through history. `Ctrl+R` opens reverse history search, like in a shell: type to filter, press `Ctrl+R` again to cycle older matches, `Tab` or `Esc` to accept into the input, `Enter` to accept and submit immediately. Running `/clear` resets the input history for the new session, though the previous session's conversation remains resumable."}, {"heading": "Multiline input", "body": "Enter submits, so multiline prompts need an escape. The universal options: type `\\` then `Enter` (works everywhere), or press `Ctrl+J` (a literal line feed, works in any terminal). `Shift+Enter` works natively in iTerm2, WezTerm, Ghostty, Kitty, Warp, Apple Terminal, and Windows Terminal; for VS Code and some others, `/terminal-setup` installs the binding. On macOS, `Option+Enter` works after configuring Option as Meta in your terminal. Pasting multi-line text (code blocks, logs) just works — paste never submits. For long-form editing, `Ctrl+G` opens your prompt in your default text editor."}, {"heading": "Prefix characters: what the first keystroke means", "body": "The first character of the input line can switch its meaning entirely. `/` invokes a command or skill. `!` enters shell mode: the command runs directly — no model interpretation, no permission prompt — and both the command and its output are added to the conversation context, meaning they land in every subsequent resend, so a `! npm test` failure is visible to Claude next turn. `@` triggers file path autocomplete, dropping a path reference into the prompt. `?` on an empty input toggles the shortcut help panel. These are harness features; the model only ever sees their results."}, {"heading": "Mode and view toggles", "body": "`Shift+Tab` cycles permission modes (`default` → `acceptEdits` → `plan`, plus any enabled optional modes) — probably the single most-used shortcut once you adopt the plan-then-execute workflow. `Ctrl+B` sends a running Bash command to the background so Claude can keep working while it runs; tmux users press it twice because `Ctrl+B` is the tmux prefix. `Ctrl+O` toggles the transcript viewer, which expands the collapsed one-line tool calls into full detail — the closest thing to watching the loop's actual traffic. `Ctrl+T` toggles Claude's task checklist. All shortcuts are provider-neutral; the terminal doesn't know or care whether the API layer underneath is Bedrock or Anthropic."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude Code docs — Interactive mode", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/interactive-mode"}, {"label": "Claude Code docs — Terminal configuration", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/terminal-config"}]}, "2.4": {"title": "Useful built-in skills & commands", "intro": "Claude Code ships two kinds of built-ins that both live behind the `/` prefix: commands that execute fixed harness logic, and bundled skills that are really just prompts. The distinction predicts cost, determinism, and what you can override.", "sections": [{"heading": "Built-in commands vs bundled skills", "body": "A built-in command like `/clear`, `/config`, or `/compact` runs deterministic code inside the CLI — same input, same behavior, and usually no tokens spent. A bundled skill like `/code-review`, `/debug`, or `/loop` is prompt-based: invoking it injects a prepared set of instructions into the conversation, and the model orchestrates the work with its tools. That means skills inherit the model's variability and cost — a skill's body enters context when invoked and stays there for the rest of the session, a recurring token cost in every resend.\n\nThe `/` menu mixes both, and the docs mark bundled skills explicitly. Quick tell: if the thing needs judgment (reviewing a diff), it's a skill; if it manipulates session state (clearing context), it's a command."}, {"heading": "The review family", "body": "Three review tools that are easy to conflate: `/code-review` is a bundled skill that reviews your local working diff for bugs and cleanup opportunities before you commit. `/security-review` checks the pending changes on the current branch specifically for security vulnerabilities. `/review` targets a GitHub pull request rather than your local diff — a fast single-pass PR review. In current versions, `/code-review` and `/verify` run only when you explicitly invoke them, so these longer, token-hungry checks never fire on their own. Since they're skills, a review pass is a real model workload — reading the diff, chasing context — billed like any other agentic work, on Bedrock straight to your AWS account."}, {"heading": "/init and memory-style workflows", "body": "`/init` has Claude explore the codebase and write a `CLAUDE.md` — the project's standing memory. This connects directly to the keystone: the model retains nothing between sessions, so \"remembering\" in Claude Code always means writing to a file that the harness injects into future context. `/memory` opens your `CLAUDE.md` files for editing at their different scopes (user-level `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`, project-level, directory-level). Because `CLAUDE.md` content rides along in essentially every request, the discipline is to keep it to durable facts and conventions; procedures that only occasionally apply belong in a skill, whose body loads only when used."}, {"heading": "Diagnostics and workflow utilities", "body": "`/doctor` runs a setup checkup: installation health, settings problems, and an estimate of what your skill listing costs in context. `/debug` is a troubleshooting skill for when Claude Code itself misbehaves. `/export` writes the conversation out as plain text. `/tasks` lists background shells and subagents. Larger workflow skills include `/batch` for orchestrating large-scale parallel changes and `/loop` for running a prompt on a recurring interval. None of these depend on the provider — the whole built-in surface is harness-side and identical whether the API layer underneath is Bedrock or Anthropic direct."}, {"heading": "Overriding and disabling built-ins", "body": "Bundled skills are the lowest layer in name resolution: a skill with the same name at the enterprise, personal (`~/.claude/skills/`), or project (`.claude/skills/`) level replaces the bundled one. Drop a `code-review` skill into your project and `/code-review` now runs your team's checklist instead of the stock one — a clean way to standardize review criteria in a repo. To switch bundled skills off wholesale, set `disableBundledSkills` in settings (everything except `/doctor` disappears). Individual skills can be tuned with the `skillOverrides` setting — hidden entirely, or collapsed to name-only in the model's skill listing to save context. Built-in commands, by contrast, aren't override-able files; they're part of the CLI itself."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude Code docs — Commands reference", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/commands"}, {"label": "Claude Code docs — Extend Claude with skills", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/skills"}]}, "2.5": {"title": "Invocation & flags", "intro": "How you start `claude` decides what kind of process you get: an interactive REPL, a one-shot headless job for CI, or a resumed session. Flags and environment variables — where Bedrock users do most of their configuration — shape the session before the first token is sent.", "sections": [{"heading": "Interactive vs print mode", "body": "`claude` starts the interactive session; `claude \"fix the failing test\"` starts it with an initial prompt already submitted. `claude -p \"query\"` (`--print`) is the headless form: run the agentic loop, print the result, exit — the mode for CI pipelines and scripts. Stdin pipes in as context: `cat error.log | claude -p \"explain this\"`. Print mode is the same harness and the same loop, just without the TUI, so permissions, settings files, `CLAUDE.md`, and MCP config all still apply. Useful companions in `-p` mode: `--output-format json` or `stream-json` for machine-readable output, and `--max-turns N` to cap how many agentic turns a run may take."}, {"heading": "Continuing and resuming: -c and -r", "body": "`claude -c` (`--continue`) reloads the most recent conversation in the current directory; `claude -r <id-or-name>` (`--resume`) picks a specific session. Both compose with `-p`, so a script can continue a prior conversation headlessly: `claude -c -p \"now check for type errors\"`.\n\nWhy this works at all is the keystone in action: nothing lives server-side between turns. Every session is durably recorded as a transcript on disk under `~/.claude/projects/`, and resuming just means the harness reads that transcript back and resends it as the conversation history on your next turn. `--fork-session` resumes under a new session ID, leaving the original untouched — cheap branching, because a \"session\" is only a local file plus whatever you resend."}, {"heading": "Model and system prompt flags", "body": "`--model` accepts an alias (`sonnet`, `opus`, `haiku`) or a full model name. On Bedrock, full names are Bedrock inference profile IDs like `us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6`, or an application inference profile ARN — and most Bedrock shops pin models via environment variables instead of per-invocation flags. `--fallback-model` names a model to fall back to automatically when the primary is unavailable.\n\nSystem prompt flags come in two strengths: `--append-system-prompt` adds your text to the end of the default system prompt, keeping all of Claude Code's tool instructions intact; `--system-prompt` replaces the entire thing — powerful and usually wrong for normal use, since you lose the harness's own operating instructions. Either way you're editing the stable prefix of every request, which also means a fresh cache write on first use."}, {"heading": "Permission and scope flags", "body": "`--permission-mode <mode>` sets the starting permission mode (`plan` is the common one). `--dangerously-skip-permissions` starts in `bypassPermissions` — reserve it for containers and throwaway VMs. `--allowedTools` and `--disallowedTools` take permission-rule syntax, e.g. `--allowed-tools \"Bash(git log *)\" \"Read\"`, pre-approving or denying specific tools for the session; this is how headless CI runs are made both useful and bounded. `--add-dir ../lib` grants file access to directories beyond the working directory. `--mcp-config` loads MCP servers from a JSON file, with `--strict-mcp-config` ignoring all other MCP sources — handy for reproducible CI environments."}, {"heading": "Env-var driven config: where Bedrock users live", "body": "The Bedrock integration is switched on and steered almost entirely by environment variables. `CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK=1` routes the API layer to Bedrock; credentials come from the AWS default credential chain (profile, SSO, access keys, or `AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK`); the region resolves from `AWS_REGION`, then `AWS_DEFAULT_REGION`, then the active profile's config. `ANTHROPIC_MODEL` sets the primary model to a Bedrock ID or inference profile ARN, and `ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_SONNET_MODEL` / `ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_OPUS_MODEL` / `ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_HAIKU_MODEL` pin what the aliases resolve to — important for team rollouts, so an alias doesn't silently move to a model your account can't invoke.\n\nYou don't have to export these in your shell: the `env` block of a settings file (e.g. `~/.claude/settings.json`) sets them per user or per project. Precedence follows the general rule that flags beat environment, which beats settings-file defaults. `/status` confirms what actually resolved."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude Code docs — CLI reference", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/cli-reference"}, {"label": "Claude Code docs — Claude Code on Amazon Bedrock", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/amazon-bedrock"}, {"label": "Claude Code docs — Settings", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/settings"}]}, "3.1": {"title": "Prompt assembly", "intro": "The model is stateless, so every turn the harness must build the entire request from scratch and send all of it. Prompt assembly is that build — and it is an act of selection against a budget, not a dump of everything on disk.", "sections": [{"heading": "The layered build", "body": "Everything the model sees lands in exactly one of three destinations in the API request. First the `tools` parameter: every tool definition — name, description, input schema. Second the `system` parameter: the system prompt (a few thousand tokens), with environment info and your `CLAUDE.md` memory riding at or near it. Note that `system` is a separate top-level field, not a message. Third the `messages` array: the entire prior conversation, tool results, harness-injected reminders, and — last of all — the thing you just typed.\n\nThis build happens on every single model call, not once per session. A single prompt from you can trigger dozens of tool-use round-trips, and each one re-assembles and re-sends the whole thing. Identical mechanics on Bedrock and the Anthropic API: the harness assembles the same Anthropic-shaped request either way; only the door it goes through differs."}, {"heading": "The stability gradient", "body": "Why that order — tools, then system, then messages? Because prompt caching matches a prefix, from the first byte of the request forward. The assembly is therefore arranged as a stability gradient: the most stable content first, the most volatile last. Tool schemas rarely change mid-session; the system prompt and `CLAUDE.md` change occasionally; the message array grows every turn.\n\nWith stable content up front, the long unchanging head is a cache hit on every call, billed at roughly 0.1x instead of 1x. Put anything volatile early — a timestamp in the system prompt, a tool list that reorders — and you invalidate everything after it, re-billing the whole prefix as a cache write at 1.25x. The ordering is not an API convention; it is load-bearing cost engineering. In real sessions, cache reads routinely cover over 90% of all input tokens — that discount is what makes the full resend economically survivable."}, {"heading": "Injection is selection, not a dump", "body": "The naive model — \"the harness concatenates system + tools + memory + history and ships it\" — misses a step: curation. Some content is always present (the system prompt, tool schemas, `CLAUDE.md`, skill names and descriptions). Other content is pulled in per turn, conditionally: a skill body loads only when its description matches what you're doing; `system-reminder` blocks appear around your messages carrying harness state; plugin selectors can match several candidate skills against your prompt and inject only some of them.\n\nAssembly runs against a token budget, and candidates that don't make the cut are dropped before the request is built. So what the model sees genuinely changes turn to turn — and there is a second-order cost: a busy, dynamic context is not just bigger, it is less cacheable, because anything that changes the stable head moves the cache boundary earlier and re-bills the prefix behind it."}, {"heading": "The transcript is not the message array", "body": "It is tempting to think the session file at `~/.claude/projects/<slug>/<id>.jsonl` is the conversation the harness sends. It isn't. The transcript is a superset: alongside `user` and `assistant` records it interleaves a dozen other record types — mode switches, permission changes, queued prompts, file-history snapshots, auto-generated titles. UI and harness state share the tape with the conversation.\n\nEach turn, the harness reads that tape, selects a subset to become the message array, then adds things that were never on the tape at all — injected reminders marked as meta content, authored by the harness rather than by you or the model. Durable record and assembled request are two different objects. This is why resuming a session is a reconstruction, not a restore: the harness replays the transcript but re-assembles the request now, with today's date, the current tool list, and fresh reminders — not byte-identical to what a live session would have sent."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude Code docs — Manage Claude's memory", "url": "https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/claude-code/memory"}, {"label": "Claude Docs — Prompt caching", "url": "https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/prompt-caching"}]}, "3.2": {"title": "The tool protocol", "intro": "The model cannot do anything — it can only ask. A tool call is structured text sent out, matched by an id, and answered on the next request. Everything that actually touches your machine is the harness.", "sections": [{"heading": "The round-trip: tool_use out, tool_result back", "body": "When Claude \"reads a file,\" no file is read by the model. The model emits a `tool_use` content block — an `id`, a tool `name`, and a JSON `input` — and then its turn ends. There is no callback, no open socket, no waiting on I/O. The harness parses that block, executes the tool locally, and starts a fresh, longer request in which the answer rides as a `tool_result` block carrying the same id as `tool_use_id`.\n\nThree details do all the work. The id is the entire correlation mechanism — the model may emit several `tool_use` blocks in one reply (parallel calls), and only the echoed id says which result answers which request. The result comes back as a `user` message — there is no dedicated tool role in this protocol. And the pairing is an invariant: every `tool_use` must be answered by a matching `tool_result` on the next request, or the API rejects the conversation as malformed. On disk you can verify the counts match exactly."}, {"heading": "System tools vs custom and MCP tools", "body": "Who defines the schema, and who executes? For built-in tools (`Read`, `Bash`, `Edit`, and friends), the harness defines the schema and the harness executes. For MCP tools, an external server defines the schema — the harness discovers it at startup, forwards it into the `tools` parameter, and when the model calls the tool, the harness relays the call to the server process, which executes and returns the result.\n\nTwo constants hold across all of them. From the model's perspective there is no difference: every tool is just an entry in the `tools` parameter and every call is the same `tool_use`/`tool_result` round-trip. And execution is always client-side — the model and the API server never run anything; the executor is either the harness process or a server the harness talks to. That also means every tool definition costs input tokens on every call whether or not it is ever used, which is why the harness can defer rarely-used MCP schemas until searched for."}, {"heading": "Why prompt injection is a protocol category, not a bug", "body": "Put two protocol facts together. First: tool output re-enters the conversation as a `user` message — the same role your instructions arrive in. Second: the model reads the message array as one undifferentiated stream and cannot tell which user-role bytes you typed and which a `Read` pulled off disk or a fetch pulled off the web.\n\nSo a file, web page, or command output that contains instructions is structurally indistinguishable from you issuing those instructions. That is prompt injection, and it is not an implementation hole — it is a direct consequence of the protocol having one input channel. When a colleague asks \"why can't the model just ignore instructions inside a file it read?\", the answer is not \"better training\": the file's contents and your prompt occupy the same role in the same array, and there is no line for the model to hold. Mitigations therefore live in the harness — permission prompts, sandboxing, not piping untrusted text into an agent with broad tool access — never in a channel distinction the protocol does not have."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude Docs — Tool use overview", "url": "https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/agents-and-tools/tool-use/overview"}]}, "3.3": {"title": "The special local files", "intro": "All durable state in Claude Code lives on your disk — the model holds none, and the server holds only an ephemeral cache. These files are what the harness reads each turn to reconstruct the request, so knowing what lives where tells you what the model can and cannot see.", "sections": [{"heading": "CLAUDE.md and rules: additive, request-shaping", "body": "`CLAUDE.md` is memory the model actually receives: it lands in the system layer of the request and is re-injected on every call, which is also why it survives compaction. It exists at several scopes — user-level at `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`, project-level at the repo root, and per-directory files deeper in the tree.\n\nThe resolution rule is the one people get wrong: scopes do not override each other, they stack. Asking \"which CLAUDE.md wins?\" is a category error — global, project, and subdirectory files are all concatenated into context at once, so a project file adds to your personal preferences rather than replacing them. That additive merge is the opposite of how `settings.json` resolves. The practical consequence: every line in every in-scope CLAUDE.md is paid for on every request of every session, so these files reward being short, specific, and genuinely universal — anything situational belongs in a skill body that loads on demand instead."}, {"heading": "settings.json tiers and precedence", "body": "Settings resolve the opposite way: by precedence, highest layer wins. The chain, from strongest to weakest: managed/enterprise policy, then CLI arguments, then the project's local file (`.claude/settings.local.json` — personal, meant to be gitignored), then the shared project file (`.claude/settings.json` — committed for the team), then the user-global `~/.claude/settings.json`.\n\nFor overlapping scalar keys — model choice, environment variables, output style — the higher layer simply discards the lower. Permissions inside these files are the exception: allow/deny rules combine across layers safety-first, with a deny anywhere beating an allow anywhere. And critically, `settings.json` is harness-shaping configuration: it governs the loop — which model to call, what to permit, which hooks to run — but its contents never cross the wire. Putting coding rules in `settings.json` and wondering why Claude ignores them is the classic confusion: the model never receives that file. Rules the model must act on belong in `CLAUDE.md`."}, {"heading": "The ~/.claude inventory: durable vs ephemeral", "body": "State lives in three tiers. Durable: your disk. Reconstructed: the message array, rebuilt in memory each turn from durable sources and thrown away. Ephemeral: the server-side prompt cache, TTL-evicted and never yours to inspect. Only the first tier is on disk to inventory.\n\nUnder `~/.claude/`: `projects/` holds session transcripts (typically the bulk of the space) plus subagent and plugin logs; `skills/` and `plugins/` hold extension code; `file-history/` holds the edit snapshots backing rewind/undo; plus `todos/`, `shell-snapshots/`, `commands/`, and `settings.json`. Beside the directory — not in it — sits `~/.claude.json`, holding global state and, for Anthropic-direct users, OAuth tokens (Bedrock users authenticate via AWS credentials instead, so no Anthropic token lives there). Two things worth saying out loud: it is all plaintext — anything you pasted into a session sits there in the clear — and it is mostly log, an append-only record the harness reads from, not \"memory the model can use.\" Retention is governed by `cleanupPeriodDays` (default 30) but is uneven across stores; if you need to know what is still on disk, look."}, {"heading": "The transcript *.jsonl and resume mechanics", "body": "Each session appends to `~/.claude/projects/<project-slug>/<session-id>.jsonl`, one JSON record per line. Only a fraction of the record types are conversation (`user`, `assistant`); the rest interleave harness and UI state — mode changes, permission-mode changes, queued prompts, file-history snapshots, generated titles. Every assistant record also carries the API's own `usage` accounting — input tokens, cache reads, cache writes, output — which is how you can audit the stateless resend from your own disk: cache reads on call n roughly equal total input on call n-1.\n\n`--continue` reopens the most recent session for the project; `--resume` lets you pick one. Both are reconstructions, not restores: the harness replays the durable transcript to rebuild the message array, then assembles a fresh request with the current date, tool list, and reminders. That is one reason a long-idle resume takes a cache miss — the server cache has expired and the rebuilt prefix no longer matches. Last caveat: the JSONL schema is undocumented and changes between versions, so treat any specific field layout as observed, not guaranteed."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude Code docs — Manage Claude's memory", "url": "https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/claude-code/memory"}, {"label": "Claude Code docs — Settings", "url": "https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/claude-code/settings"}]}, "3.4": {"title": "Configuration surfaces", "intro": "Every configuration surface answers two independent questions: resolution — when several layers set it, which wins — and landing — does it change the request the model sees, or only how the harness behaves. Hold both axes and most \"why is Claude ignoring my config?\" mysteries dissolve.", "sections": [{"heading": "Axis 1 — Resolution: three rules, not one", "body": "There is no single precedence rule; different surfaces resolve differently, and mixing them up is a real bug source. Override: `settings.json` scalars (model, env, output style) follow the precedence chain — managed policy beats CLI args beats local project settings beats shared project settings beats user-global — and the winning layer discards the rest. Additive: `CLAUDE.md`, skills, and MCP servers do not compete across scopes; global, project, and directory contributions all concatenate into context at once. Deny-wins: permissions combine safety-first — a deny at any layer beats an allow at any other, regardless of which layer is \"higher.\"\n\nSo \"which CLAUDE.md wins?\" is a category error (they stack), \"which permission wins?\" has a fixed answer (the deny), and \"which model applies?\" follows precedence. Three surfaces, three resolution rules — diagnose config by first asking which rule the surface uses."}, {"heading": "Axis 2 — Landing: request vs harness", "body": "The load-bearing axis. Request-shaping config lands in the payload the model receives: `CLAUDE.md` (system layer), MCP tool definitions (`tools` parameter), skill names and descriptions (bodies only on invoke), output-style and system-prompt appends. It costs tokens on every call and its churn can move the cache boundary. Harness-shaping config governs the loop and never crosses the wire: model selection, permission rules, hooks, retention settings, UI preferences.\n\nThe test: if the model must see it to act on it, it is request-shaping; if it governs what the harness does, it is harness-shaping — and harness config is invisible to the model, so no wording makes the model \"obey\" a setting it never receives. This predicts both classic confusions at once: rules in `settings.json` get ignored because they never reach the model, and an idle MCP server still costs tokens because its schemas land in every request. A hook costs zero context; a skill description costs some on every call."}, {"heading": "Env vars, settings files, and flags", "body": "The same key can often be set three ways, differing in lifetime and precedence. Settings files are durable defaults, resolved by the precedence chain. CLI flags (`--model`, `-p`, `--resume`) apply to one invocation and sit above the settings files in the chain. Environment variables are per-process: they come from your shell, and settings files can also pin them via their `env` block so every session sees them.\n\nOn the landing axis, almost all env vars and flags are harness-shaping — they steer which endpoint, model, and behavior the harness uses, not what the model reads. Remember also that settings are durable config that shape the reconstructed request; they are not themselves in it. The request is rebuilt each turn according to them: edit a settings file or `CLAUDE.md` mid-session and the effect appears when the harness next re-resolves that surface, not by any message to the model."}, {"heading": "The Bedrock delta: config is env-var heavy", "body": "On the Anthropic API you `/login` once and most configuration can live in settings files. On Bedrock, the provider switch itself is environment configuration: `CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK=1` tells the harness to sign requests with AWS credentials (from your standard AWS credential chain — profiles, SSO, IAM roles) instead of an Anthropic key, and region selection comes from your AWS environment. There is no `/login` step; auth is whatever your AWS environment provides.\n\nModel selection shifts too: instead of short Anthropic model names, you point at Bedrock model IDs or inference profiles, typically via environment variables such as `ANTHROPIC_MODEL`. A team standardizing on Bedrock usually pins all of this in the `env` block of a checked-in `.claude/settings.json`, so the provider wiring rides the same precedence chain as everything else. The crucial invariant: all of this is harness-shaping. The assembled request — tools, system, messages — is identical either way; Bedrock is a different door to the same model, and every mechanism in topics 3.1–3.3 applies unchanged."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude Code docs — Settings", "url": "https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/claude-code/settings"}, {"label": "Claude Code docs — Claude Code on Amazon Bedrock", "url": "https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/claude-code/amazon-bedrock"}]}, "3.5": {"title": "Permissions", "intro": "Permissions are the harness's gate on what the model's proposals are actually allowed to do. The model only ever emits a `tool_use` request; whether that request executes is decided entirely on your machine, by rules the model never sees.", "sections": [{"heading": "The gate lives in the harness, not the model", "body": "Every action in Claude Code follows the same protocol: the model returns a `tool_use` block, and the harness decides whether to execute it. Permission rules sit at that decision point. They are harness-shaping configuration — they govern the loop and are never transmitted in the request payload, so they cost zero tokens and are invisible to the model.\n\nThis invisibility cuts both ways. You cannot make the model 'respect' a permission by asking nicely in a prompt, because the model never receives the rule — the harness simply blocks or allows after the fact. But equally, no clever prompt injection in a file or web page can talk the gate open, because the gate is not prompt-addressable. That is exactly why permissions are enforced in the harness rather than delegated to model judgment: they hold even when the model is confused or manipulated. Everything here is provider-neutral — the gate runs locally and works identically on Bedrock and the Anthropic API."}, {"heading": "Allow, deny, ask — and deny always wins", "body": "Rules take the form `Tool(specifier)`: `Bash(npm run test:*)`, `Read(~/.zshrc)`, `WebFetch(domain:example.com)`. Each rule lands in one of three lists — `allow` (run without asking), `deny` (never run), `ask` (always prompt) — and rules can live at every settings tier: managed/enterprise, project `.claude/settings.json`, project-local `settings.local.json`, and user-level.\n\nThe resolution model is the part people get wrong. Scalar settings like `model` resolve by precedence — the highest layer wins and lower layers are discarded. Permissions do not. Rules from all layers combine, and a `deny` at any layer beats an `allow` at any other layer. It is safety-first merging, not a precedence ladder. So if your user settings allow `Bash(rm:*)` but the project denies it, the command is blocked — no tier outranks a deny. Claude Code has three distinct resolution regimes across its surfaces: override (settings scalars), additive (CLAUDE.md stacking), and deny-wins (permissions). Applying the wrong regime to the wrong surface is a classic misconfiguration."}, {"heading": "Permission prompts and where answers persist", "body": "When a proposed action matches no rule, the harness pauses the loop and prompts you. Your answer can apply once, for the rest of the session, or 'always' — and 'always' is just rule-writing: the harness appends a rule to a settings file (typically the project's `settings.local.json`, which is meant to stay out of version control). Next time, the rule matches and no prompt appears.\n\nThis matters most in headless mode. `claude -p` has no human to prompt, so an unmatched action can't be interactively approved — non-interactive runs need their allowlist configured up front, a permission mode that doesn't prompt, or they will stop at the gate. If you find yourself approving the same command daily, that is a signal to promote the decision into a durable allow rule rather than re-answering the prompt."}, {"heading": "Permission modes", "body": "Modes shift where the prompting threshold sits without changing the rule machinery. Default mode prompts for anything not pre-allowed. Accept-edits mode auto-approves file edits (the routine bulk of prompts) while still gating commands. Plan mode goes the other direction: the harness restricts Claude to read-only exploration — it can search and read but not mutate, which is why plan mode is the safe way to let it roam an unfamiliar codebase. Bypass-permissions mode skips prompting entirely and should be confined to isolated, disposable environments such as containers and CI, where the blast radius is bounded by the environment itself.\n\nThe key mental model: modes adjust the *ask* behavior; explicit rules still exist underneath. A mode is a session-level dial, while rules are durable configuration — and both are pure harness state, never part of the request."}, {"heading": "Sandboxing: a second, OS-level layer", "body": "Permission rules answer 'may this tool call run at all?' Sandboxing answers a different question: 'given that it runs, what can it touch?' Sandboxed execution uses operating-system isolation primitives to confine commands — restricting filesystem writes outside the workspace and limiting network access — independently of the allow/deny layer.\n\nThe two layers compose. A command can be permitted by rules yet still be unable to write outside the project because the sandbox stops it at the OS level. This is also why sandboxing can *reduce* prompt fatigue: when the harness knows a command is confined, it can afford to ask less often, because even a bad command has a bounded blast radius. Rules are policy enforced by the harness process; the sandbox is containment enforced by the OS beneath it. Defense in depth means a mistake has to get through both."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude Code docs — Identity and access management (permissions)", "url": "https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/claude-code/iam"}, {"label": "Claude Code docs — Settings", "url": "https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/claude-code/settings"}]}, "3.6": {"title": "Context lifecycle & compaction", "intro": "Within a session, context only ever accumulates — it never resets on its own. Compaction is the one mechanism that bounds that growth, and it only makes sense once you remember the keystone: every turn re-sends the entire conversation.", "sections": [{"heading": "Why compaction must exist", "body": "The model is stateless, so the harness rebuilds and re-sends the whole conversation on every turn. Two consequences follow directly. First, cost: turn *n* pays for everything from turn 1 to *n*, so a session's cumulative token bill grows roughly quadratically with its length. Second, a hard wall: the model's context window is finite, and a session that keeps accumulating — prompts, replies, and above all verbose tool outputs — will eventually hit the ceiling and simply cannot continue.\n\nCaching and compaction are the two answers, and they solve different halves. Prompt caching makes the resend *cheap* (a cached prefix re-reads at roughly a tenth of base input price) but does nothing about size — a cached 180k-token context is still 180k tokens against the window. Compaction makes the resend *bounded*: it shrinks what gets assembled into future requests. You need both, and confusing them is a common quiz trap: caching is a discount, compaction is a diet."}, {"heading": "What's kept, what's lost", "body": "Compaction replaces older messages with a model-written summary while keeping your most recent exchanges and key decisions. It is lossy on purpose. The documented caveat is worth quoting almost verbatim: specific instructions from early in the conversation may not be preserved. Early verbose detail — long tool outputs, the exact wording of an instruction you gave in turn 2 — is collapsed into summary prose or dropped entirely.\n\nThat yields a practical rule: a persistent instruction does not belong in your opening prompt, because a fold can eat it. It belongs in `CLAUDE.md`, which is re-injected into every request and therefore survives every compaction — durable rules go in the always-present layer, never in the foldable one. You can even steer the fold itself: adding a note like 'when compacting, always preserve the current task list' to `CLAUDE.md` gives the summarizer guidance, since it reads your memory files when producing the summary."}, {"heading": "Auto-compact, /compact, and what a fold costs", "body": "Auto-compaction triggers as context nears the window limit (the exact threshold is not documented). Manual `/compact` runs the same fold on demand and accepts focus instructions — `/compact keep the API design decisions` — and a `PreCompact` hook can fire first, with a `trigger` field distinguishing `manual` from `auto`, giving you a chance to archive state before the fold.\n\nCompaction is not free; it costs you twice. First, the summarization itself is a model call that must read the entire conversation being folded — one full-price pass over everything, precisely because so little of a fresh summarization prompt is cacheable. Second, the fold is a deliberate cache reset: replacing old turns with a summary rewrites the prefix from that point, so the previously cached prefix is abandoned and the next request pays a large cache write (1.25x base input at the 5-minute TTL) instead of cheap reads. It is a one-time cost paid to stop re-shipping the old bulk on every future turn — a good trade exactly when the folded history was large."}, {"heading": "/clear vs compaction vs a new session", "body": "Three escalating resets, all operating on the *reconstructed* context — none of them touch the durable transcript, which is append-only on disk. Compaction even appends to it: a summary plus a `compact_boundary` marker record. Your ledger stays intact; only the working set shrinks.\n\n`/clear` is the blunt tool: it drops the conversational context entirely and continues in the same session and directory — nothing carries forward except what lives in the always-present layer (`CLAUDE.md`, settings, skills). Compaction is the surgical tool: continuity via summary, at the cost of the summarization pass. Starting a new session is `/clear` plus a fresh transcript file, and `--resume` can later rebuild any old session from its transcript — a reconstruction, not a restore.\n\nCost heuristic: when you're switching to an unrelated task, `/clear` beats compaction — you pay nothing and lose nothing you need. Compact when you need the thread of the work to continue past the window. Letting auto-compact fire mid-task is the worst timing; compacting at a natural boundary, or clearing, is deliberate context hygiene."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude Code docs — Manage costs effectively", "url": "https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/claude-code/costs"}, {"label": "Claude Code docs — Manage Claude's memory", "url": "https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/claude-code/memory"}]}, "3.7": {"title": "Mid-session changes", "intro": "Editing CLAUDE.md, settings, or MCP config mid-session raises one real question: when does the harness re-read each source? Because the request is rebuilt from scratch every turn, 'does my change take effect?' always reduces to 'is this surface re-resolved per turn, or was it fixed at session start?'", "sections": [{"heading": "The frame: config is read, not subscribed", "body": "Nothing in the system watches config and pushes updates into a live model — there is no live model to push to. The model is stateless; each turn, the harness assembles a fresh request from durable files on disk. So every mid-session-change question is really a cadence question about the assembler: which sources does it consult again on each turn, and which did it snapshot once when the session started?\n\nBroadly there are two cadences. Per-turn (or on-change) surfaces are re-consulted as the loop runs — memory files and permission rules behave this way, which is why a new deny rule can gate the very next tool call. Per-session surfaces are resolved once at startup — the tool inventory and MCP server connections are the clearest case — and editing them on disk changes nothing until a restart or an explicit reload. When a change 'doesn't take', you are almost always holding a per-session surface and expecting per-turn behavior."}, {"heading": "What re-resolves as you go", "body": "Files Claude itself edits are trivially current — the next `Read` sees the new bytes, because file content enters context through tool results, not through any snapshot. Permission rules are consulted at each gate check, so tightening or loosening them lands on subsequent tool calls without a restart. Memory (`CLAUDE.md`) edits made mid-session are surfaced to the model too — but by a telling mechanism: rather than silently rewriting the copy already sitting in the assembled context, the harness injects a notice with the updated content into the message flow.\n\nSome dials are per-request by nature: `/model` switches models for the very next call, because model choice is just a field the harness sets on each API request. Harness-side dials change freely mid-session; content already woven into history cannot be retroactively unsaid."}, {"heading": "What waits for a new session", "body": "The tool set is the canonical per-session surface. MCP servers are launched and their tool schemas collected when the session initializes; those schemas become part of the request's `tools` parameter. Editing MCP config on disk mid-session does not conjure new tools into the running loop — the harness isn't re-negotiating with servers each turn — so adding a server generally means restarting the session or explicitly reconnecting via the MCP management surface. The same session-start logic applies to the composed system prompt and the loaded plugin/skill inventory: they are established during assembly setup, not re-derived from scratch every turn.\n\nThis is also why `--resume` behaves subtly: it replays the durable transcript to rebuild history, but re-resolves the *environment* now — current date, tool list, settings. A resumed session is old conversation plus new configuration, exactly as the two cadences predict."}, {"heading": "System-reminder injection: the delivery channel", "body": "When the harness needs the model to know something changed mid-session, it uses `system-reminder` blocks: harness-authored notes injected into the message array near the current turn. File-change notices, memory updates, background-task notifications, and skill/plugin state all arrive this way. Two properties define them. They are assembly-time artifacts — the model sees them, but you never typed them, and they are not part of the durable user-authored conversation; the transcript on disk and the message array the model saw are genuinely different objects.\n\nSecond, they land at the *tail*, and that placement is load-bearing. Prompt caching matches from the start of the request: mutating the stable head (system prompt, CLAUDE.md layer, tool schemas) would invalidate the cached prefix and force an expensive rewrite. Appending a reminder near the end leaves the whole cached head intact. System-reminders are how the harness reconciles two conflicting needs — keep the prefix byte-stable for the cache, yet still deliver fresh, volatile state to a stateless model."}, {"heading": "The cache lens on every mid-session edit", "body": "Rank any mid-session change by where it lands on the stability gradient. Changes that only touch harness behavior — permission rules, hooks, mode switches — cost zero tokens and have no cache effect at all; the model never sees them. Changes delivered as tail injections — system-reminders, new user messages, tool results — cost their own tokens but preserve the cached prefix. Changes that rewrite the stable head are the expensive class: editing `CLAUDE.md` content that sits in the system layer, or changing the tool set, perturbs the prefix, and the next request pays a fresh cache write (1.25x base input) for everything after the edit point instead of ~0.1x reads.\n\nThe takeaway: mid-session config churn isn't forbidden, it's *priced*. One deliberate CLAUDE.md edit is a one-time miss, usually worth it; constant fiddling with prefix-resident config throws away the discount that makes long sessions affordable. The economics are identical on Bedrock and the Anthropic API — only cache TTL options and pricing details differ by provider."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude Code docs — Manage Claude's memory", "url": "https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/claude-code/memory"}, {"label": "Claude Code docs — Settings", "url": "https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/claude-code/settings"}]}, "3.8": {"title": "Skill injection mechanics", "intro": "Skills extend what Claude knows how to do by injecting instructions at the prompt-assembly seam. The whole design is one idea — progressive disclosure: every skill's description is always in context, but its body loads only when triggered.", "sections": [{"heading": "Descriptions always, bodies on trigger", "body": "A skill is a directory with a `SKILL.md`: frontmatter carrying a name and description, then a body of instructions (plus optional supporting files). The harness splits these two parts across two very different lifecycles. The name and description of *every* available skill ride along in *every* request — they are ambient context, because the model cannot choose to use a skill it cannot see. The body, which may be thousands of tokens of procedure, scripts, and examples, is loaded only when the skill actually triggers.\n\nThis is the resolution of an otherwise impossible trade-off. Ship all bodies always and a few dozen installed skills would devour the context window before any work began; ship nothing and the model could never discover that a skill exists. Progressive disclosure pays a small permanent tax (descriptions) to defer the large cost (bodies) until proven necessary — the same shape as MCP's deferred tool schemas, and for the same reason."}, {"heading": "Where a skill lands in the assembly", "body": "Prompt assembly is layered, stable-first: system prompt and tool schemas, then context files, then history, then the current turn. Skill *descriptions* live in the stable ambient layer — part of the prefix that stays byte-identical turn over turn, which means the cache absorbs their cost: after the first write, they re-read at ~0.1x base input price on every subsequent call.\n\nA triggered skill *body* lands in the opposite place: it is injected into the message flow near the current turn, in the volatile tail, arriving via the harness's injection machinery rather than by rewriting the prefix — so invoking a skill does not disturb the cached head. One under-appreciated consequence: once injected, the body becomes part of the conversation history, and the stateless loop re-sends history every turn. A 3,000-token skill body invoked at turn 10 is paid for (at cache-read rates once stable) on turns 11, 12, 13... until a compaction folds it or `/clear` drops it. Skill bodies are loaded on demand, but they don't unload on their own."}, {"heading": "The two trigger paths", "body": "Skills trigger two ways. Model-triggered: the model reads the ambient descriptions, decides the current task matches one, and invokes it — the harness then loads the body into context and the model follows it. User-triggered: you type the skill's slash name and force the load directly. Either way, the mechanics converge: trigger, inject body, proceed with the instructions in context.\n\nThe practical corollary is that the description is the routing function. Model-triggered activation is only as reliable as the description's match against real task phrasings — a vague description means a skill that never fires (or fires spuriously), no matter how good its body is. That's why skill authoring guidance obsesses over descriptions: they are the only part the model sees at decision time. Note also that some skills delegate their actual work to a subagent, which runs in a separate loop with its own context and returns only results — combining skill injection with context isolation so a heavy procedure never bloats the parent conversation at all."}, {"heading": "The cost model, and how skills compare to their neighbors", "body": "Three cost tiers, worth memorizing as a contrast set. Hooks: zero tokens ever — they run in the harness process and never touch the payload. Custom slash commands: zero *ambient* tokens — a command file is injected into messages only when you type `/name`; an unused command is free. Skills: a permanent per-request description tax for every installed skill, plus the body cost on trigger and its re-send cost thereafter.\n\nThe description tax is small per skill and largely cache-absorbed — but only while the skill set is *stable*. Descriptions sit in the prefix, so installing or removing a skill perturbs the cached head and forces a fresh cache write on the next request. A churning skill inventory is quiet cache poison. And sprawl compounds: dozens of installed skills means a permanently fatter prefix and a harder routing problem for the model. The discipline follows directly from the mechanics: keep the always-on set lean, push rarely-used procedures into commands (zero ambient cost) or subagent-backed skills (isolated context), and treat every new skill as a standing line item on every future request, not a one-time install."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude Code docs — Skills", "url": "https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/claude-code/skills"}, {"label": "Claude Code docs — Manage costs effectively", "url": "https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/claude-code/costs"}]}, "4.1": {"title": "Hooks", "intro": "Hooks are the loop-native extension point: commands the harness runs at named events in the agent loop. They fire deterministically in the harness process — the model never gets a vote on whether they run.", "sections": [{"heading": "Callbacks at seams of the loop", "body": "There is one agent loop: assemble prompt, send, execute tools, resend. Hooks are callbacks registered at named seams of that loop. When the event fires, the harness runs your command on your machine — not on Anthropic's or AWS's servers, and not inside the model. That placement gives hooks three properties. First, they cost zero context by default: a hook that only observes adds nothing to the prompt. Second, they can short-circuit the loop: a hook can stop a tool call before it executes. Third, they are provider-neutral — hooks never touch the API layer, so they behave identically on Bedrock and the Anthropic API.\n\nHooks resolve a tension in the configuration story: they are harness-side machinery that can also produce request-shaping content. A `UserPromptSubmit` hook runs in the harness, yet its stdout can be injected into the prompt the model sees. Hooks are the bridge that turns loop events into payload."}, {"heading": "The event map", "body": "The core events trace the loop itself. `SessionStart` fires when a session begins or resumes. `UserPromptSubmit` fires before Claude processes your prompt. `PreToolUse` and `PostToolUse` bracket every tool call. `Stop` fires when Claude finishes a turn; `SubagentStart` and `SubagentStop` bracket delegated work. `PreCompact` fires before context compaction, and `Notification` covers things like permission prompts.\n\nTool events accept a matcher that filters by tool name: an exact string (`Bash`), alternatives (`Edit|Write`), a regex (`mcp__github__.*` for all tools from one MCP server), or `*` for everything. Other events match on their own dimension — `SessionStart` on the source (`startup`, `resume`, `clear`, `compact`), `PreCompact` on `manual` vs `auto`. Some events, like `UserPromptSubmit` and `Stop`, take no matcher and always fire."}, {"heading": "Exit codes: the blocking contract", "body": "A hook communicates through its exit code. Exit 0 means success: the action proceeds. Normally stdout goes only to the debug log, but for `UserPromptSubmit` and `SessionStart` it is added as context the model can see — that is how hooks inject.\n\nExit 2 is the blocking signal, and stderr is fed back to Claude as the explanation. What blocking means depends on the event: `PreToolUse` cancels the tool call and hands the model the rejection instead; `UserPromptSubmit` rejects the prompt; `Stop` prevents Claude from stopping, forcing it to continue; `PreCompact` blocks compaction. Events that report something already finished — `PostToolUse`, `Notification` — cannot un-happen it; their exit-2 feedback is advisory.\n\nAny other exit code is a non-blocking error: execution continues and the failure is noted. This three-way contract (0 / 2 / other) is the part worth memorizing."}, {"heading": "JSON control beyond exit codes", "body": "For finer control, a hook exiting 0 can print structured JSON to stdout. A top-level `{\"decision\": \"block\", \"reason\": \"...\"}` blocks with an explanation on events that support it. `PreToolUse` hooks get the richest interface: `hookSpecificOutput.permissionDecision` can be `allow` (auto-approve, skipping the permission prompt), `deny`, or `ask` (escalate to the user), and the hook can even rewrite the tool's input before it runs. A `PostToolUse` hook can replace the tool's output — for example, redacting secrets before they enter context. The universal field `\"continue\": false` stops Claude entirely, regardless of event.\n\nThis JSON channel is what turns hooks from tripwires into policy engines: allow `npm test` silently, deny writes to production paths, route everything else to the human."}, {"heading": "Where hooks live", "body": "Hooks are configured in the same settings tiers as everything else: `~/.claude/settings.json` (all your projects), `.claude/settings.json` (shared with the team via version control), `.claude/settings.local.json` (project-private), managed policy files (organization-wide), and plugins. The structure nests event → matcher group → hook list, where each hook entry has a `type` (usually `command`), the command itself, and an optional `timeout` (default 600 seconds for commands). Run `/hooks` to see every configured hook by event and source.\n\nBecause hooks execute arbitrary commands with your credentials, treat project-scoped hooks in a cloned repository like any other code you are about to run: read them before trusting the workspace."}, {"heading": "Hooks vs asking the model nicely", "body": "You can write \"always run the formatter after editing\" in `CLAUDE.md`, and the model will usually comply — but instructions in context are probabilistic. They compete with everything else in the prompt, they can be compacted away, and a long session degrades adherence. A `PostToolUse` hook on `Edit|Write` that runs the formatter is guaranteed: it fires every time, in the harness, whether or not the model remembers.\n\nThe rule of thumb: use hooks for invariants that must hold every time (formatting, lint gates, blocking dangerous commands, audit logging) and context instructions for things requiring judgment (style preferences, architectural taste). If the sentence starts with \"always\" or \"never\", it probably wants to be a hook."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude Code docs — Hooks reference", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks"}]}, "4.2": {"title": "Skills", "intro": "Skills intervene at the assembly seam of the loop: they inject instructions into the prompt. The trick is progressive disclosure — the model always knows a skill exists, but pays for its body only when it is used.", "sections": [{"heading": "What a skill is", "body": "A skill is a directory containing a `SKILL.md` file: YAML frontmatter between `---` markers, then Markdown instructions. The frontmatter's `description` tells Claude when the skill applies; the body is what Claude follows once it loads. The directory name becomes the command — `.claude/skills/deploy-staging/SKILL.md` creates `/deploy-staging`.\n\nThe directory can hold more than `SKILL.md`: templates, example outputs, executable scripts, reference docs. `SKILL.md` should reference these so Claude knows what each contains and reads them only when needed — keep the entrypoint under roughly 500 lines and push detail into supporting files. Create a skill when you keep pasting the same procedure into chat, or when a section of `CLAUDE.md` has grown from a fact into a procedure."}, {"heading": "Progressive disclosure — the cost model", "body": "This is the mechanism that makes skills different from `CLAUDE.md`. Skill descriptions are always in context, so Claude can decide when one is relevant; skill bodies load only on invocation. A 2,000-word deployment runbook costs a one-line description per turn until the day you actually deploy.\n\nContrast with `CLAUDE.md`, which rides in the prompt on every single turn — and because the harness resends the entire conversation each turn (the stateless loop), every always-present token is paid again and again. Skills convert that recurring cost into a pay-on-use cost.\n\nOne asymmetry to know: once invoked, the rendered skill content enters the conversation as a message and stays for the rest of the session — it is not re-read from disk on later turns, and it does not unload. The lazy part is loading, not lifetime."}, {"heading": "Where skills live and who wins", "body": "Three levels: personal (`~/.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`, all your projects), project (`.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`, checked into the repo), and plugin (`<plugin>/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`, namespaced as `plugin-name:skill-name` so it cannot collide with the others).\n\nOn a name clash, enterprise-managed skills override personal, and personal overrides project. A skill at any level also overrides a bundled skill of the same name — a project `code-review` skill replaces the built-in `/code-review`. Legacy `.claude/commands/` files participate too, but a skill beats a same-named command.\n\nBecause project skills arrive with a cloned repository and can request tool permissions via frontmatter, they take effect only after you accept the workspace trust dialog — review them like code."}, {"heading": "Who invokes: you, the model, or both", "body": "By default a skill is both user-invocable (`/name`) and model-invocable (Claude loads it when the description matches the task). Two frontmatter switches change that.\n\n`disable-model-invocation: true` means only you can trigger it. Use this for workflows with side effects — `/deploy`, `/commit`, `/send-slack-message` — where you want to control timing; you don't want Claude deciding to deploy because the code \"looks ready\". As a bonus, the description is dropped from the always-loaded listing, costing zero context until used.\n\n`user-invocable: false` is the mirror image: only Claude can invoke it, and it is hidden from the `/` menu. Use it for background knowledge that is not a meaningful user action — a `legacy-system-context` skill Claude should consult when relevant."}, {"heading": "Frontmatter beyond triggering", "body": "Several fields shape how a skill runs. `allowed-tools` pre-approves listed tools for the turn that invokes the skill — Claude can run them without permission prompts, and the grant clears on your next message (the skill's text persists; its permissions do not). `model` switches models for the rest of the current turn — route a mechanical task to a cheaper model. `argument-hint` documents expected arguments in autocomplete. `paths` restricts automatic loading to work on matching files.\n\nThe most structural field is `context: fork`, which runs the skill in its own subagent rather than inline — its work happens in a separate context window and only the result returns, with `agent` choosing the subagent type. That is one seam invoking another: a skill (assembly seam) offloading to a subagent (the offload seam)."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude Code docs — Extend Claude with skills", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/skills"}]}, "4.3": {"title": "Custom Commands", "intro": "Custom slash commands are the smallest extension point: a Markdown file that becomes a reusable prompt template. In current Claude Code they have been merged into skills — same machinery, simpler packaging.", "sections": [{"heading": "Commands are skills now", "body": "A file at `.claude/commands/deploy.md` and a skill at `.claude/skills/deploy/SKILL.md` both create `/deploy`, support the same frontmatter, and work the same way. Existing `.claude/commands/` files keep working; user-level commands live in `~/.claude/commands/`. If a command and a skill share a name, the skill wins.\n\nWhat the merge means mechanically: typing `/deploy` expands the file's content into your prompt — a template substitution at the assembly seam, before anything is sent. The file's body becomes instructions in the current turn's message. There is no separate \"command engine\"; a command is a single-file, user-triggered skill."}, {"heading": "Arguments", "body": "Commands accept arguments after the name: `/fix-issue 417` makes the text `417` available inside the template via `$ARGUMENTS`, so the file can say \"Fix GitHub issue $ARGUMENTS following our conventions\". The `argument-hint` frontmatter field (for example `[issue-number]`) documents the expected shape in the autocomplete menu.\n\nBecause expansion happens before the request is assembled, arguments are pure text substitution — the model sees only the final expanded prompt, never the template. That makes commands deterministic in a way instructions are not: the same invocation always produces the same injected text."}, {"heading": "When a command beats a full skill", "body": "Reach for a bare command file when the whole procedure fits in one prompt, you always trigger it yourself, and it needs no supporting files: commit-message conventions, a standard review checklist, a boilerplate generator.\n\nGraduate to a skill directory when you need what the merge added: supporting files (scripts, templates, reference docs loaded on demand), invocation control (`disable-model-invocation` for side-effect workflows, or letting Claude auto-load it when relevant), tool pre-approval via `allowed-tools`, or `context: fork` to run in a subagent. The upgrade path is mechanical — move `deploy.md` into `deploy/SKILL.md` and the `/deploy` command is unchanged."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude Code docs — Skills (custom commands section)", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/skills"}, {"label": "Claude Code docs — Commands reference", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/commands"}]}, "4.4": {"title": "MCP", "intro": "The Model Context Protocol adds a third tool executor to the loop. The model still just emits `tool_use` blocks; MCP changes who executes them — an external server instead of the harness or the provider.", "sections": [{"heading": "A third executor", "body": "Recall the tool protocol: the model emits a `tool_use` block, something executes it, and a `tool_result` comes back as the next user message. Who executes? Built-in tools (`Bash`, `Read`, `Edit`) run in the harness on your machine. Server-side tools (like web search) run on the provider's infrastructure inside the request. MCP adds the third executor: an external server — a local process or a remote service — that the harness routes matching `tool_use` calls to.\n\nEach connected server advertises its tools at startup; the harness folds their schemas into the `tools` parameter of the API request, so from the model's point of view an MCP tool is indistinguishable from a built-in. Three executors, one protocol. That symmetry is why MCP integrates so cleanly — nothing about the loop changes."}, {"heading": "Transports", "body": "Four transports, two that matter most. `stdio` runs the server as a local child process — the config is a `command`, `args`, and `env`; ideal for tools needing direct system access. `http` is the recommended transport for remote servers (`claude mcp add --transport http name https://...`); the MCP spec calls it `streamable-http`, and Claude Code accepts that alias in JSON config. `sse` (Server-Sent Events) is deprecated in favor of HTTP. `ws` (WebSocket) exists for servers that push events unprompted, but supports only header auth and cannot be added via the `--transport` flag.\n\nOne config gotcha: a JSON entry with a `url` but no `type` is read as a stdio server and skipped with an error — remote entries must declare their transport."}, {"heading": "Three config scopes", "body": "Where a server definition lives determines who gets it. Local scope (the default) stores the server in `~/.claude.json` under the current project's path — private to you, in this project only. Project scope writes `.mcp.json` at the project root, designed to be checked into version control so the whole team shares the configuration. User scope (`--scope user`) makes a server available to you across all projects.\n\nPrecedence on a name clash: local beats project beats user.\n\nBecause a cloned repository's `.mcp.json` could point at anything, Claude Code prompts for approval before using project-scoped servers, and a repo cannot approve its own servers via committed settings — approval is yours. `claude mcp reset-project-choices` clears those decisions."}, {"heading": "Tool naming, auth, and limits", "body": "MCP tools follow the naming convention `mcp__<server>__<tool>` — for example `mcp__github__create_pr`. This full name is what you use in permission rules, hook matchers (`mcp__github__.*`), and subagent `tools` lists. Plugin-bundled servers get a longer form: `mcp__plugin_<plugin>_<server>__<tool>`.\n\nRemote servers that require OAuth 2.0 authenticate through the `/mcp` panel, which walks the browser flow and stores tokens; simpler setups pass API keys as headers or env vars in the server config.\n\nTwo context protections matter. Tool schemas cost tokens on every turn (the full resend), so the harness can defer schemas — tools appear by name only and load on demand via `ToolSearch`. And tool outputs are capped: 25,000 tokens by default with a warning at 10,000, raisable via `MAX_MCP_OUTPUT_TOKENS`. A chatty server cannot silently flood your window."}, {"heading": "The Bedrock angle", "body": "MCP is entirely harness-side plumbing. The server list, transports, approvals, and routing never touch the model API — so everything on this page works identically whether Claude Code talks to Bedrock or to the Anthropic API. `CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK` changes the door the harness sends requests through; it does not change how tools execute.\n\nThe subtlety worth flagging: an MCP server is its own program, and some servers make their own model calls internally (a summarization server, an agent-in-a-server). Those calls use the server's credentials and provider, not yours — your Bedrock configuration does not propagate into an external process. If governance requires all model traffic to stay inside AWS, you must vet each MCP server's own behavior separately; the harness only guarantees where its traffic goes."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude Code docs — Connect to tools via MCP", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp"}]}, "4.5": {"title": "Subagents", "intro": "A subagent is not the model \"thinking harder\" — it is a whole separate agent loop with its own context window. Only its final report crosses back to the parent. That pass-by-value boundary is the entire point.", "sections": [{"heading": "A separate loop, pass by value", "body": "When Claude uses the `Agent` tool, the harness spawns a child agent loop: its own system prompt, its own context window, its own transcript, its own tool calls, its own permission checks. The child sees none of the parent's conversation history — it gets only the task prompt the parent wrote, plus basic environment details like the working directory.\n\nWhen the child finishes, its final text report returns to the parent as a single `tool_result`. Everything else — every file it read, every search result, every intermediate step — stays in the child's context and dies with it (the transcript persists on disk, but not in the parent's window).\n\nThat is pass-by-value semantics: the parent receives a copy of the conclusion, never a reference into the child's working memory. If the child didn't put it in the report, the parent will never know it."}, {"heading": "Why isolation is the point", "body": "Connect this to the keystone: the harness resends the parent's entire conversation every turn. Any token that enters the parent's context is paid again on every subsequent API call for the rest of the session. Fifty thousand tokens of grep output read inline is not a one-time cost — it is a recurring tax, and it drags the parent toward compaction.\n\nDelegate that search to a subagent and the parent's context grows only by the returned summary — a few hundred tokens instead of fifty thousand. The exploration happened, the conclusion arrived, the bulk never touched the parent's resend.\n\nThe corollary cuts the other way: when the details themselves must inform your next edits — the exact code you are about to modify — read them inline. A summary of code is not code. Delegate high-volume, low-retention reading; keep load-bearing detail in the main loop."}, {"heading": "The built-ins", "body": "Claude Code ships with built-in subagents it delegates to automatically. `Explore` is the read-only search agent — Claude sends it broad codebase questions and gets conclusions back without flooding the main context; the caller specifies thoroughness (quick / medium / very thorough). `Plan` does read-only research during plan mode, keeping exploration out of the main window while the conversation stays non-mutating. `general-purpose` gets the full tool set for multi-step tasks that mix exploration and modification.\n\nA detail with cost implications: `Explore` and `Plan` skip loading your `CLAUDE.md` files to keep research fast and cheap; other subagents load them. And delegation itself is description-driven — Claude matches the task against each agent's `description` field, which is why writing that field well is the main lever over when your custom agents get used."}, {"heading": "Custom agent definitions", "body": "A custom subagent is a Markdown file with YAML frontmatter, in `.claude/agents/` (project, check it in) or `~/.claude/agents/` (personal, all projects); plugins and a `--agents` CLI flag are additional sources, and the definition closest to your working directory wins on a name clash.\n\nOnly `name` and `description` are required. The Markdown body becomes the child's system prompt — it replaces, not extends, the default. Frontmatter shapes the sandbox: `tools` restricts the tool list, `model` pins a model (route mechanical work to Haiku), `permissionMode` sets its permission behavior, `maxTurns` caps runaway loops, `skills` preloads full skill bodies at startup, and `isolation: worktree` gives the agent a temporary git worktree so its edits cannot collide with yours.\n\nThe pattern: encode a repeatable role — code reviewer, test runner, migration auditor — once, with exactly the tools and model that role needs."}, {"heading": "Foreground, background, and reporting", "body": "Subagents now run in the background by default: the parent keeps working while the child runs, and the child's report arrives later as a completion notification the parent consumes on a subsequent turn. Foreground runs block until done — Claude uses them when it needs the result before continuing. Permission prompts from either mode surface in your main session, naming the subagent that asked; a child cannot approve anything on your behalf.\n\nTwo operational notes. Background subagents get a reduced built-in tool set compared to foreground ones — the same definition can resolve to different tools depending on where it runs. And a subagent can be resumed or sent follow-up messages, continuing with its context intact — useful when the first report raises a follow-up question, because asking the same child is cheaper than briefing a new one from scratch."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude Code docs — Create custom subagents", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/sub-agents"}]}, "4.6": {"title": "Agent Teams & Orchestration", "intro": "Agent teams scale past the subagent model: instead of children reporting to one parent, multiple full Claude Code sessions coordinate as peers — a lead plus teammates with a shared task list and direct messaging.", "sections": [{"heading": "From one loop to many sessions", "body": "A subagent is a child loop inside your session that can only report back to its caller. An agent team is a different topology: the main session becomes the team lead, and each teammate is a separate, full Claude Code session — its own context window, its own loop, loading the project context (`CLAUDE.md`, MCP servers, skills) like any fresh session would. The lead's conversation history does not carry over; a teammate knows only its spawn prompt plus what the project provides.\n\nTeams are experimental and disabled by default — enable with `CLAUDE_CODE_EXPERIMENTAL_AGENT_TEAMS=1` in your environment or `settings.json`. Once enabled, you describe the parallel work in natural language and the lead spawns teammates; you can also select any teammate in the agent panel and message it directly, without going through the lead."}, {"heading": "The coordination machinery", "body": "Two shared structures replace the parent-child reporting line. The shared task list (stored under `~/.claude/tasks/`) holds work items with three states — pending, in progress, completed — and dependency links; a task with unresolved dependencies cannot be claimed. The lead assigns tasks, or teammates self-claim the next unblocked one, with file locking preventing two teammates from grabbing the same task.\n\nMessaging runs through mailboxes: each agent has a JSON inbox at `~/.claude/teams/{team-name}/inboxes/`, and any teammate can message any other by name — the peer-to-peer channel subagents lack.\n\nHooks extend into this layer as quality gates: `TeammateIdle` fires when a teammate is about to go idle (exit 2 sends feedback and keeps it working), and `TaskCreated` / `TaskCompleted` can block task transitions — a deterministic \"definition of done\" enforced in the harness."}, {"heading": "Teams vs subagents — choosing", "body": "The decision rule: do the workers need to talk to each other? Subagents are cheaper and simpler — results are summarized back into the caller's context, and the main agent does all coordination. Use them when only the conclusion matters: research, verification, focused implementation of a spec.\n\nTeams earn their overhead when the work benefits from peer discussion: parallel code review with different lenses (security, performance, test coverage), debugging by competing hypotheses where teammates actively try to disprove each other's theories, or cross-layer features where frontend, backend, and test owners must negotiate interfaces as they go.\n\nFor sequential work, same-file edits, or heavily dependent steps, both are the wrong tool — a single session outperforms a team that spends its tokens on coordination. Practical guidance from the docs: start with 3–5 teammates and 5–6 tasks each."}, {"heading": "Parallel work needs isolated files", "body": "Teammates run in the same working directory by default, and nothing merges concurrent edits — two teammates writing the same file simply overwrite each other. The first defense is decomposition: split the work so each teammate owns a disjoint set of files.\n\nThe stronger defense is worktree isolation. Git worktrees give each parallel agent its own checkout of the repository on its own branch, sharing the same underlying repo; edits land in separate directories and integrate later through normal git merges. The same idea appears one level down in the subagent world as `isolation: worktree` frontmatter, which spawns the agent in a temporary worktree that is cleaned up automatically if nothing changed.\n\nThe invariant to carry: parallelism in the model layer is easy; parallelism in the filesystem is where fan-out actually breaks. Isolate the filesystem, not just the context windows."}, {"heading": "Cost multiplication — and Bedrock quotas", "body": "Every teammate is a full session running the full stateless-loop economics: its own context, its own per-turn resend of everything it has accumulated. Token usage therefore scales roughly linearly with team size — five teammates approach five sessions' worth of spend, plus coordination messages on top.\n\nOn Bedrock this multiplies against your account's service quotas, not a per-seat plan. Fan-out means N sessions issuing concurrent requests against shared tokens-per-minute and requests-per-minute quotas in your region — a team that runs smoothly on the Anthropic API can throttle on on-demand Bedrock, surfacing as `ThrottlingException` errors and stalled teammates that stop mid-task. Before running teams on Bedrock, check your quotas against the expected concurrency, consider provisioned throughput for sustained workloads, and remember that pointing teammates at a cheaper model reduces cost but not request-rate pressure."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude Code docs — Orchestrate agent teams", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-teams"}, {"label": "Claude Code docs — Create custom subagents", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/sub-agents"}]}, "4.7": {"title": "Dynamic Workflows", "intro": "There are two ways to orchestrate multi-agent work: let the model decide the shape in-loop, or write a script that imposes the shape from outside. Knowing which to reach for is an architecture decision, not a tooling one.", "sections": [{"heading": "Two orchestration styles", "body": "Model-driven orchestration happens inside a session: Claude reads the task, decides whether to spawn subagents or teammates, picks how to split the work, and adapts as results come back. It is flexible and zero-setup — and probabilistic. The same prompt can produce different decompositions on different runs.\n\nDeterministic orchestration inverts control: a script you write (a shell pipeline calling headless `claude -p` per stage, or a program built on the Agent SDK) owns the sequencing. Each stage is a fresh invocation — its own stateless loop, its own context — and the script handles ordering, retries, gating, and what flows between stages, typically as files or captured output. The model does the work inside each stage; the structure between stages is code, and code does not improvise."}, {"heading": "When structure beats one big context", "body": "A long multi-stage job run in a single session accumulates everything: stage one's exploration is still being resent — or has been lossily compacted — while stage four executes. Costs grow superlinearly and early details blur.\n\nA pipeline resets that. Each stage starts a fresh context containing only its inputs: analyze writes `findings.md`, implement reads it, verify checks the diff. Handing artifacts through files instead of shared context is the same pass-by-value discipline subagents enforce — applied between whole invocations rather than within one.\n\nDeterminism also composes with the other seams: hooks and plain exit codes make honest gates between stages (tests must pass before the next `claude -p` fires), which no in-context instruction can guarantee."}, {"heading": "Choosing — and mixing", "body": "Reach for model-driven orchestration when the decomposition itself needs judgment: exploratory debugging, open-ended research, work where the right split only emerges as findings arrive. Reach for a script when the workflow is known, repeatable, and must be auditable — CI review gates, nightly migrations, batch refactors across many repos — where you want the same stages in the same order every run, with machine-checkable checkpoints.\n\nThe strongest pattern is hybrid: deterministic skeleton, dynamic interior. The script fixes the stages and the gates; within a stage, the model still delegates to subagents as it sees fit. Structure where you need guarantees, model judgment where you need adaptation."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude Code docs — Create custom subagents", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/sub-agents"}, {"label": "Claude Code docs — Agent SDK: the agent loop", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/agent-loop"}]}, "5.1": {"title": "Auth methods", "intro": "Claude Code speaks one protocol but can walk through several doors: Anthropic direct, AWS Bedrock, Google Vertex, or an enterprise gateway. Which door you use changes how you authenticate — and almost nothing else.", "sections": [{"heading": "Bedrock: AWS credentials, not API keys", "body": "On Bedrock there is no `/login` and no Anthropic API key. You set `CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK=1` and Claude Code authenticates the way any AWS SDK client does: it resolves the default AWS credential provider chain. That chain checks, in order, environment variables (`AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID`/`AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`/`AWS_SESSION_TOKEN`), a named profile (`AWS_PROFILE`, including SSO profiles via `aws sso login`), and instance/container roles. There is also a shortcut: an Amazon Bedrock API key exported as `AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK`, which skips the chain entirely.\n\nEach request is then signed with AWS SigV4 and sent to the Bedrock runtime endpoint in your region. Because auth is AWS-native, `/logout` is meaningless on Bedrock — your identity is your AWS credentials, and they expire and refresh on AWS's schedule, not Claude Code's. Settings like `awsAuthRefresh` can run `aws sso login` automatically when credentials expire mid-session."}, {"heading": "IAM is the permission surface", "body": "On Bedrock, what you may do is decided by IAM policy, not by an Anthropic account. The minimum useful policy grants `bedrock:InvokeModel` and `bedrock:InvokeModelWithResponseStream` on the foundation-model and inference-profile ARNs you use (plus `bedrock:ListInferenceProfiles`/`GetInferenceProfile` so Claude Code can discover and resolve profiles).\n\nTwo Bedrock-only gates exist before any of this works: your account must have Anthropic model access enabled in the Bedrock console (a one-time use-case form per AWS account), and the model must be available in your chosen region. Region resolution follows `AWS_REGION`, then `AWS_DEFAULT_REGION`, then your AWS profile's region. This is the governance appeal of Bedrock: credentials, scoping, rotation, and audit all ride existing AWS machinery (IAM, CloudTrail), with no separate vendor account to manage."}, {"heading": "Anthropic direct: API key or subscription OAuth", "body": "Going direct, there are two identities. An API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, or created via the Claude Console) bills pay-per-token against an Anthropic organization. Alternatively, `/login` runs an OAuth flow against a Claude subscription (Pro/Max), so usage draws from the plan's included allowance instead of metered API billing.\n\nThe key contrast with Bedrock: direct auth is a bearer credential Claude Code itself manages and stores, while Bedrock auth is ambient AWS identity the harness merely borrows. That is why a Bedrock user's `settings.json` and environment are full of `AWS_*` and `CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK` variables, while a direct user may have configured nothing at all beyond `/login`."}, {"heading": "Also on the map: Vertex and gateways", "body": "Google Vertex AI is the third first-class door: `CLAUDE_CODE_USE_VERTEX=1` plus Google Cloud credentials (gcloud auth), with its own project and region settings — structurally the Bedrock story with GCP nouns.\n\nEnterprises often add a fourth pattern: an LLM gateway or proxy. Claude Code points its base URL at the gateway (`ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL`, or `ANTHROPIC_BEDROCK_BASE_URL` for a Bedrock-shaped gateway), and the gateway injects the real credentials server-side. That centralizes keys, quotas, and logging, and developers never hold provider credentials at all. In every case the invariant holds: only the transport and identity change — the request payload, the tool protocol, and the stateless loop are identical through every door."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude Code docs — Claude Code on Amazon Bedrock", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/amazon-bedrock"}, {"label": "AWS docs — Identity and access management for Amazon Bedrock", "url": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/security-iam.html"}]}, "5.2": {"title": "Model identity & availability", "intro": "The same Claude weights sit behind every provider, but each provider names and gates them differently. Knowing how a model ID is spelled — and why Bedrock adds prefixes and suffixes — explains most availability surprises.", "sections": [{"heading": "Two naming schemes for one model", "body": "Anthropic direct uses short IDs like `claude-sonnet-4-5` (optionally with a date suffix pinning a snapshot). Bedrock wraps the same model in its own catalog identity: `anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929-v1:0` — vendor prefix, model, snapshot date, and a Bedrock version suffix.\n\nOn top of that sits a third form you will actually use most: the cross-region inference profile ID, e.g. `us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929-v1:0`. The geographic prefix (`us.`, `eu.`, `apac.`, `us-gov.` in GovCloud) tells Bedrock it may route your request to whichever region in that geography has capacity. Many newer Claude models on Bedrock are only served through inference profiles — invoking the bare foundation-model ID fails with \"on-demand throughput isn't supported\", which is Bedrock telling you to use the profile ID instead. Enterprises can go one step further with application inference profiles: account-specific ARNs that wrap a model for cost tracking and access control."}, {"heading": "Availability is per account, per region", "body": "Direct API access is binary: if the model exists, your key can call it. Bedrock adds two more gates. First, model access must be enabled in your AWS account (the one-time use-case submission). Second, the model must be deployed in your region — availability genuinely differs by region, and new Anthropic models can arrive on Bedrock later than on the direct API, or land in some regions before others. Feature support can lag too (for example, the 1-hour cache TTL exists only for specific models on Bedrock).\n\nCross-region inference profiles soften the regional problem by routing within a geography, but they don't remove the account-level gate. `aws bedrock list-inference-profiles --region <region>` is the ground truth for what your account can actually invoke."}, {"heading": "How Claude Code picks a model on Bedrock", "body": "Claude Code's aliases (`opus`, `sonnet`, `haiku`) resolve to built-in default Bedrock model IDs — which may not be enabled in your account. At startup Claude Code verifies the intended models are invokable and falls back (earlier version, or Sonnet when no Opus is available) with a notice if not.\n\nDeployments therefore pin models with environment variables: `ANTHROPIC_MODEL` for the primary, and `ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_OPUS_MODEL` / `ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_SONNET_MODEL` / `ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_HAIKU_MODEL` to steer each alias, all set to inference profile IDs or application-inference-profile ARNs. A `modelOverrides` map in `settings.json` can route each model version in the `/model` picker to its own ARN. On the direct API, none of this exists — you pass a model name and Anthropic serves it."}, {"heading": "One consequence worth internalizing", "body": "Model identity interacts with caching and cost. The prompt cache is per-model: switching model IDs mid-session (including switching between two Bedrock profiles backed by different snapshots) shares no cached prefix and forces a full re-prefill of the conversation. And because Bedrock model choice is env-var- and settings-driven rather than interactive, a misconfigured pin quietly changes which weights — and which per-token price — every session in a team rollout is using. Pinning exact versions is how Bedrock deployments control both behavior drift and spend."}], "resources": [{"label": "AWS docs — Amazon Bedrock inference profiles", "url": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/inference-profiles-support.html"}, {"label": "Claude Code docs — Claude Code on Amazon Bedrock", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/amazon-bedrock"}, {"label": "Claude Platform docs — Models overview", "url": "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/models/overview"}]}, "5.3": {"title": "Prompt caching (the API view)", "intro": "Every turn, the harness resends the entire conversation to a stateless API. Prompt caching is the only reason that resend is affordable: the server keeps the expensive computation for the unchanged prefix and charges you ~10% to reuse it.", "sections": [{"heading": "Breakpoints and prefix matching", "body": "Caching is opt-in per request via markers: `cache_control` blocks on the Anthropic-shaped payload (which is also what Claude Code sends to Bedrock's InvokeModel API), or `cachePoint` blocks in Bedrock's Converse API. Up to 4 breakpoints per request; each says \"everything from the start of the request up to here is a reusable prefix.\" Matching is exact-prefix and order-sensitive, over the canonical ordering `tools` then `system` then `messages` — stable content first, volatile content last.\n\nA prefix must clear a per-model minimum (roughly 1,024–4,096 tokens depending on model) or it is silently not cached. In a live session the harness runs a rolling breakpoint that trails the conversation's settled tail: each turn, the newly settled chunk is written to cache once, and everything before it is read back. The signature in `usage` is small cache writes and huge cache reads, call after call. One Bedrock delta: Bedrock has no automatic caching — breakpoints must be explicit (Claude Code places them for you)."}, {"heading": "Three billing tiers", "body": "Input tokens come in three prices. Uncached input is 1x the base rate. A cache write costs a premium — 1.25x for the default 5-minute TTL, 2x for the 1-hour TTL — because the server must store the computed state. A cache read costs about 0.1x.\n\nThe economics: the write on turn n becomes the read on turn n+1 and every turn after. Break-even is roughly two reads; a long coding session reads each written chunk dozens or hundreds of times, so the write premium is rounding error. The `usage` object itemizes all of it: `input_tokens` (uncached remainder after the last breakpoint), `cache_creation_input_tokens`, `cache_read_input_tokens`. Bedrock's Converse API reports the same split as `cacheWriteInputTokens` / `cacheReadInputTokens`. In a healthy session, cache reads are the overwhelming majority of all input tokens — that 0.1x tier absorbs most of the bill."}, {"heading": "TTLs: 5 minutes vs 1 hour, and the Bedrock delta", "body": "A cache entry lives for its TTL and the TTL refreshes on every hit, so steady traffic keeps a 5-minute cache alive indefinitely at no extra charge. The risk is gaps: think time, a meeting, a long build. If no hit lands within the TTL, the entry is evicted and the next call pays full prefill for the whole conversation. The 1-hour TTL (write at 2x instead of 1.25x) is insurance against exactly that — worth it when gaps between calls routinely exceed 5 minutes.\n\nProvider delta: on the direct API the 1-hour TTL is generally available. On Bedrock the default is 5 minutes, the 1-hour option exists only for specific models (via a `ttl` field on the cache marker), and Claude Code exposes it as an opt-in (`ENABLE_PROMPT_CACHING_1H=1`). Prompt caching also isn't supported in every Bedrock region — if your cache token counts sit at zero, check the region's support table before debugging anything else."}, {"heading": "What breaks the cache", "body": "Anything that perturbs the prefix invalidates everything after the perturbation. The classic causes: adding or removing a tool (tool definitions sit first, so this dumps the entire cache); changing the system prompt, or any volatile content placed early (a timestamp is enough); editing earlier history — compaction is the big one, since it rewrites the transcript; toggling request-level settings like tool choice or thinking parameters; switching models (the cache is per-model); and idling past the TTL.\n\nA miss has a readable signature: cache reads collapse and the write column spikes as the whole prefix is re-written. The practical rule: keep the head still. A slightly larger prefix that never changes is cheaper than a smaller one that wobbles, because churn doesn't just cost its own tokens — it dumps a possibly 200k-token cached prefix and re-bills it at the write premium."}, {"heading": "What the cache physically stores", "body": "The cache is not stored text and not memory. During prefill, every transformer layer computes key/value attention vectors for each token. The cache stores that per-layer K/V state server-side, keyed by the exact token prefix. A hit loads the state instead of recomputing it — valid because identical tokens through identical weights provably produce identical vectors. That is also why one changed token invalidates everything after it: later K/V state was computed attending to the old token.\n\nThree corrections to folk models. It is not session memory — evict it and nothing is forgotten; the next call just costs more. A cache read does not mean tokens weren't sent — the full prefix crosses the wire every call; only recomputation is skipped. And it has nothing to do with `~/.claude` on your disk — it lives in the provider's serving fleet, per model, TTL-evicted."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude Platform docs — Prompt caching", "url": "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching"}, {"label": "AWS docs — Prompt caching for faster model inference", "url": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/prompt-caching.html"}]}, "5.4": {"title": "Rate limiting & quotas", "intro": "Both providers meter you in requests and tokens per minute, but the machinery differs: Anthropic uses usage tiers managed in its console; Bedrock uses AWS Service Quotas per model per region, with a token 'burndown' system that makes output tokens count far more than you'd guess.", "sections": [{"heading": "Anthropic direct: tiers, token buckets, 429s", "body": "The direct API limits three dials per model: requests per minute (RPM), input tokens per minute (ITPM), and output tokens per minute (OTPM). Limits are set by usage tier — organizations start low and move up automatically with usage history — and enforced with a token-bucket algorithm, so capacity replenishes continuously rather than resetting each minute (which also means short bursts can trip a limit your average rate wouldn't).\n\nExceeding any limit returns HTTP 429 with a `retry-after` header, plus `anthropic-ratelimit-*` headers showing the limit, remaining capacity, and reset time. Each tier also carries a monthly spend cap. The killer detail is cache-aware ITPM: for most models, `cache_read_input_tokens` do not count toward ITPM at all — only uncached input and cache writes do. With a high cache-hit rate, your effective throughput is several times the printed limit, which is one more reason a stable cached prefix matters."}, {"heading": "Bedrock: Service Quotas, per model, per region", "body": "On Bedrock there are no Anthropic tiers. Your AWS account has quotas per model per region — tokens per minute (TPM), tokens per day (TPD), and requests per minute — visible and (where marked adjustable) raisable through the AWS Service Quotas console. Defaults vary by account age, payment history, and region; new accounts start reduced.\n\nExceeding a quota surfaces as a `ThrottlingException` from the Bedrock runtime, which the AWS SDK (and Claude Code) retries with exponential backoff. Two relief valves are Bedrock-specific: cross-region inference profiles spread load across a geography's regions, and provisioned throughput lets you buy dedicated model capacity (in \"model units\") for a fixed hourly commitment instead of sharing the on-demand pool — you then invoke via the provisioned ARN. On-demand plus a quota-increase request covers most Claude Code teams; provisioned throughput is for sustained, predictable heavy load."}, {"heading": "The burndown rate: why output tokens are so expensive against quota", "body": "Bedrock's TPM/TPD quotas count input and output together, but not equally. Output tokens burn down at a model-specific multiplier — 5x for most Claude models (10x or more for some newer ones) — so one generated token consumes multiple quota tokens. Cache reads consume no quota at all; uncached input and cache writes count 1:1. Billing is unaffected: you pay for actual tokens; burndown only governs throttling.\n\nThe accounting is done in three steps: at request start, Bedrock deducts total input plus your full `max_tokens` from quota (throttling you if that overdraws); during generation it adjusts; at the end it settles to input + cache writes + output x burndown, refunding the unused reservation. Consequence: an inflated `max_tokens` throttles concurrent requests even if the model never generates that much — a quota problem invisible in your bill. This whole mechanism has no direct-API equivalent, where `max_tokens` explicitly does not factor into OTPM."}, {"heading": "Same failure, different shapes — and what agents do to quotas", "body": "Functionally, both providers give you the same experience: a throttle error, a backoff, a retry. The deltas are in shape and remedy. Direct: 429 + `retry-after`, raise limits via the Console's request flow or by aging into a higher tier. Bedrock: `ThrottlingException`, raise limits via Service Quotas requests, region choice, cross-region profiles, or provisioned capacity — and quota is a shared account resource, so Claude Code competes with every other Bedrock workload in the account and region.\n\nAgentic workloads stress this more than chat: one user turn can be dozens of API calls in a tight loop, and subagent fan-out multiplies concurrent calls. On Bedrock the burndown math means a team running parallel agents can exhaust a TPM quota while the AWS bill still looks modest. When throttling appears mid-session, the harness's retries usually absorb it — sustained throttling is the signal to look at quotas, not code."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude Platform docs — Rate limits", "url": "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/api/rate-limits"}, {"label": "AWS docs — Quotas for Amazon Bedrock", "url": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/quotas.html"}, {"label": "AWS docs — How tokens are counted in Amazon Bedrock", "url": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/quotas-token-burndown.html"}]}, "5.5": {"title": "Costs", "intro": "Claude Code cost has one root cause — the stateless loop resends the whole conversation every call — and one set of levers. Everything else is arithmetic over four token prices.", "sections": [{"heading": "The four token prices", "body": "Every API call is billed from its `usage` object across four classes. Uncached input: 1x the model's base input rate. Cache write: 1.25x (5-minute TTL) or 2x (1-hour TTL) — paid once per newly cached chunk. Cache read: about 0.1x — paid every time a cached prefix is reused. Output: several times the input rate (roughly 5x is the standard shape), and it includes `thinking` tokens, which you never see as prose but pay for as output.\n\nPer-token rates also differ by model tier — Opus-class costs several times Sonnet-class, which costs several times Haiku-class — and Bedrock prices Claude at rates published on the Bedrock pricing page, billed through AWS. In a healthy cached session the bill is dominated by two lines: the mass of cache reads at 0.1x, and the output tokens at the top rate."}, {"heading": "Why session cost grows superlinearly", "body": "The API is stateless: turn n resends turns 1 through n-1 plus everything stable (system prompt, tool schemas, `CLAUDE.md`). So per-call input cost grows with conversation length, and total session cost grows roughly quadratically — turn 41 re-ships and the server re-reads what turn 40 already read. And a \"turn\" is not one call: the agentic loop makes a model call per tool round-trip, so a single user request can be dozens of calls, each carrying the full array.\n\nCaching converts most of that quadratic mass to the 0.1x tier — it makes the resend survivable, not free. The tell is in `usage`: cache reads on call n approximately equal total input on call n-1. That correspondence is the resend, in the provider's own accounting. It is also why one broken cache late in a long session (a tool added, a model switched) is a visible cost event: the whole accumulated prefix re-bills at the write premium."}, {"heading": "Three billing regimes", "body": "Who receives your money differs by door. Anthropic API key: pay-per-token against an Anthropic organization, tracked in the Claude Console with its spend caps and workspace limits. Claude subscription (Pro/Max via `/login`): a flat monthly fee with a usage allowance — no per-token metering visible to you, but hard usage windows instead.\n\nOn Bedrock — the regime this reader lives in — there is no Anthropic account anywhere. Token charges land on the AWS bill like any other service, priced per model from the Bedrock pricing page. That means AWS-native cost tooling: Cost Explorer, cost allocation tags, per-account or per-application-inference-profile attribution, budgets and alerts. It also means procurement, compliance, and spend governance ride existing AWS agreements — a major reason enterprises choose this door. The trade: no subscription flat rate exists on Bedrock; every token is metered."}, {"heading": "The cost levers, ranked", "body": "First, protect the cache: keep the prefix stable (don't churn MCP servers or tool sets mid-session), keep gaps shorter than the TTL or buy the 1-hour TTL where available — cache stability routinely matters more than raw prompt size.\n\nSecond, manage context length, because it compounds: `/clear` between unrelated tasks resets the resend to near zero; compaction trims a long session but itself costs a full read of the conversation and breaks the cache once, so let it happen at natural boundaries rather than mid-flow.\n\nThird, model choice: routing routine work to a cheaper tier (Sonnet or Haiku instead of Opus) cuts every token class at once — on Bedrock, an unpinned default silently deciding this for you is a real budget risk.\n\nFourth, subagent isolation: a subagent explores in its own context and returns only a summary, so a 50k-token investigation doesn't join the main conversation and get resent every turn thereafter. The inverse also holds: parallel fan-out multiplies concurrent sessions, cost, and Bedrock quota pressure together."}], "resources": [{"label": "Amazon Bedrock pricing", "url": "https://aws.amazon.com/bedrock/pricing/"}, {"label": "Claude Platform docs — Prompt caching (pricing tiers)", "url": "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching"}]}, "5.6": {"title": "Request/response anatomy", "intro": "Underneath every provider is the same Anthropic-shaped Messages payload: a messages array in, content blocks out, a stop reason deciding what the loop does next. Bedrock wraps that payload in AWS transport — it doesn't change it.", "sections": [{"heading": "The request: one array, rebuilt every call", "body": "A request carries: the `model`, a required `max_tokens`, an optional `system` prompt, the `tools` array of JSON-schema definitions, and `messages` — alternating `user` and `assistant` entries containing the entire conversation so far, rebuilt by the harness from scratch every call. Sampling knobs (`temperature`, `top_p`, `stop_sequences`) ride alongside. The `tools`-then-`system`-then-`messages` ordering is load-bearing for caching: stable material first, the live turn last.\n\nBedrock delta: with the InvokeModel API — which is what Claude Code uses — the model is not in the body at all; it's the resource in the request URL (as a model ID or inference profile ARN). The body is the native Anthropic payload plus one Bedrock-specific field, `anthropic_version: \"bedrock-2023-05-31\"`, and no API-key header — the request is SigV4-signed instead."}, {"heading": "The response: typed content blocks", "body": "The assistant message comes back as a list of typed content blocks, not a string. `text` is prose for the human. `thinking` is reasoning, billed as output. `tool_use` is a structured request to run a tool — it carries an `id`, a `name`, and JSON `input`, and crucially the server does not execute it; it is just text the harness parses and acts on locally.\n\nThe fourth block type travels the other way: `tool_result`, which the harness sends back keyed by `tool_use_id` — inside a `user` message. That detail is structural: file contents, command output, and human speech all arrive on the single user channel, wearing the same role. This is why prompt injection via tool results is a protocol-level category rather than a bug. Alongside the blocks, every response carries the `usage` object — the per-call token accounting that all cost and cache analysis reads from."}, {"heading": "Stop reasons drive the loop", "body": "Each response ends with a `stop_reason`, and the harness's control flow is keyed directly to it. `tool_use` means the model paused for tool results — the harness executes, appends a `tool_result`, and calls again; this is the agentic loop's engine, and one user turn can cycle it dozens of times. `end_turn` means the model finished naturally — the loop exits and control returns to you. `max_tokens` means the output was truncated at your cap, not a natural ending — the response may be mid-sentence or mid-tool-call. `stop_sequence` means a caller-supplied string was generated.\n\nNote the two-layer stop distinction: a stop reason ends one model call (one decode); only a reply without `tool_use` ends the harness turn. Conflating them is the most common misreading of the loop."}, {"heading": "Streaming: same events, different envelope", "body": "Both providers stream, because decode produces tokens one at a time and nobody wants to wait for the last one. The direct API uses server-sent events: `message_start`, `content_block_start`, `content_block_delta` chunks carrying text fragments, through `message_delta` (which carries the stop reason) and `message_stop`.\n\nBedrock delivers the equivalent event sequence through `InvokeModelWithResponseStream`, but wrapped in AWS's binary event-stream framing (content-type `application/vnd.amazon.eventstream`) rather than SSE. Same information, different envelope — and the envelope is a real operational concern: a corporate gateway that transparently re-encodes the binary stream as SSE, or rewrites its content-type, breaks Claude Code's stream parsing. The class of \"works direct, fails through our proxy\" bugs on Bedrock is usually this."}, {"heading": "InvokeModel vs Converse: Bedrock's two shapes", "body": "Bedrock exposes two request shapes for the same models. InvokeModel is a passthrough: the body is the model vendor's native format — for Claude, the Anthropic Messages payload nearly verbatim. Converse is AWS's normalized, model-agnostic schema — camelCase fields, `toolSpec` tool definitions, `cachePoint` blocks instead of `cache_control`, and usage reported as `inputTokens`/`cacheReadInputTokens`/`cacheWriteInputTokens` — designed so one integration can swap vendors.\n\nClaude Code uses the InvokeModel API, not Converse. That is the deep reason the harness behaves identically across providers: the payload it assembles — the ordering, the cache breakpoints, the tool schemas — is the same bytes-on-the-wire either way, with Bedrock contributing only the URL, the signature, and the stream framing. Bedrock is a different door into the same protocol, not a different protocol."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude Platform docs — Messages API reference", "url": "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/api/messages"}, {"label": "AWS docs — Anthropic Claude Messages API on Bedrock", "url": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/model-parameters-anthropic-claude-messages.html"}, {"label": "Claude Code docs — Claude Code on Amazon Bedrock", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/amazon-bedrock"}]}, "6.1": {"title": "What a model is", "intro": "Strip away the chat interface and a language model is a very large file of numbers plus a small amount of code that does arithmetic with them. Holding that picture makes almost everything else in this branch — size, speed, cost, the context window — follow logically.", "sections": [{"heading": "Weights are numbers in memory", "body": "A model is billions of learned numbers called weights (or parameters), produced once during training and then frozen. Inference — answering your prompt — is not lookup or search; it is arithmetic. Your text is converted to tokens, the tokens flow through layer after layer of matrix multiplications against those weights, and out comes a probability distribution over possible next tokens.\n\nOn the serving side, the weights are loaded into accelerator memory once, read-only, and shared: every user's request flows through the same weights. There is no per-user copy and no per-user state living inside the model between calls. That is the physical root of the keystone fact about Claude Code: the API is stateless, so the harness must resend the entire conversation every turn."}, {"heading": "Parameters vs file size vs capability", "body": "Three different numbers get conflated. Parameter count is how many weights the model has — a \"200B model\" has roughly 200 billion of them. File/memory size is parameter count times how many bits store each weight: size (GB) ≈ params (billions) × bits-per-weight ÷ 8. So the same model can occupy very different amounts of memory depending on storage precision (that knob is quantization, topic 6.6).\n\nCapability correlates with parameter count but not linearly, and not automatically: training data, training technique, and architecture matter enormously, and a well-trained smaller model routinely beats a poorly-trained larger one. Parameter count tells you about capacity and about serving cost — it never guarantees quality on your task."}, {"heading": "The context window is a hard budget", "body": "The context window is the maximum number of tokens the model can attend over in a single call — system prompt, tool schemas, conversation history, and the reply all inside one budget. It is not a memory that fills gradually across a session; because of the stateless resend, every single call must fit the whole assembled prompt inside it.\n\nThis is why the window is a hard engineering constraint, not a soft preference: as a Claude Code session grows, each turn's resend grows with it, and when the assembled prompt approaches the limit the harness must compact — summarize and drop history — or the next call simply cannot be made. The window caps a call, and therefore caps how much conversation can survive verbatim."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude platform docs — Glossary", "url": "https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/about-claude/glossary"}]}, "6.2": {"title": "The read phase (prefill)", "intro": "A model call is not one operation but two: prefill (read the whole prompt) then decode (write the reply token by token). Prefill is where most of the compute goes, and it is exactly the work that Claude Code's full-resend loop repeats every turn — which makes it the key to understanding caching, input pricing, and time-to-first-token.", "sections": [{"heading": "Two phases, not one box", "body": "The mental model \"input → model → output\" hides the only distinction that matters for cost and latency. When a request arrives, the server first runs prefill: it processes the entire input prompt to build up internal state. Only then does decode begin, generating the reply one token at a time against that state.\n\nThe two phases have opposite performance characters. Prefill scales with input length and is compute-bound; decode scales with output length and is memory-bandwidth-bound (topic 6.3). Nearly all the raw work in a typical Claude Code call is prefill, because the assembled prompt — system prompt, tool schemas, CLAUDE.md, the whole conversation so far — is usually tens of thousands of tokens, while the reply is far shorter. Turn 41 re-reads turns 1–40, which turn 40 already read; prefill is what the resend repeats."}, {"heading": "Parallel ingestion, compute-bound", "body": "Prefill's superpower is parallelism. All input tokens are already known, so the model can process them simultaneously: at every layer, each token's computation runs alongside every other token's, saturating the accelerator with dense matrix multiplications. Modern hardware is exceptionally good at exactly this shape of work, so prefill chews through input at rates orders of magnitude faster than decode emits output.\n\n\"Compute-bound\" means the limiting factor is arithmetic throughput — the chips are genuinely busy multiplying — rather than waiting on memory transfers. This is why a 100,000-token prompt can be ingested in a couple of seconds while generating 100,000 tokens of output would take many minutes: reading is parallel, writing is serial. Whenever you notice that pasting a huge file into a session barely slows the response start, you are seeing prefill parallelism at work."}, {"heading": "Prefill builds the KV cache", "body": "Prefill is not just reading — it produces an artifact. For every input token, every layer computes and stores a Key vector and a Value vector: the attention state that later tokens will consult to decide which earlier tokens matter. This per-layer K/V store is the KV cache, and it is the working memory that decode reads on every generated token.\n\nPrompt caching (the API feature) is this artifact made persistent. The provider keys stored K/V state by the exact token prefix; a cache read loads that state instead of recomputing it — valid because same tokens through same weights provably produce identical vectors. The state is order-sensitive: each token's K/V was computed attending to everything before it, so one changed token early in the prompt invalidates all state after it. That is why the harness orders the prompt stable-prefix-first, and why cache reads (about 0.1× input price) save compute, not bandwidth — the tokens still cross the wire."}, {"heading": "Why input is cheap and prefill sets time-to-first-token", "body": "The pricing asymmetry — output tokens costing roughly five times input tokens — falls out of the two phases. An input token is one slice of a massively parallel batch computation; an output token requires its own full serial pass through the model. Per token, prefill is simply far cheaper to serve, and the pricing reflects the physics.\n\nPrefill also explains the latency you feel. Time-to-first-token is dominated by how long prefill takes on the uncached part of your prompt: nothing can be generated until the whole input has been read. A long prompt with a warm cache starts answering almost immediately (the K/V for the cached prefix is loaded, only the new suffix prefills for real); the same prompt cold pays full prefill first. So in a Claude Code session, the pause before text starts streaming is the read phase, and the speed of streaming afterwards is the write phase."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude platform docs — Prompt caching", "url": "https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/prompt-caching"}, {"label": "Claude platform docs — Glossary", "url": "https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/about-claude/glossary"}]}, "6.3": {"title": "The write phase (decode)", "intro": "After prefill has read the prompt, decode writes the answer — one token at a time, each requiring its own full pass through the model. Decode's serial, memory-hungry nature explains why output tokens cost more, why replies stream instead of appearing at once, and why long outputs dominate wall-clock time.", "sections": [{"heading": "One token at a time, unavoidably", "body": "Generation is autoregressive: the model predicts the next token, appends it to the sequence, and only then can it predict the one after — token n+1 mathematically depends on token n. Unlike prefill, where every input token is known up front and can be processed in parallel, decode has no parallelism to exploit along the sequence. A 2,000-token reply is 2,000 sequential forward passes, full stop.\n\nThis is why responses stream: tokens genuinely come into existence one by one, and sending each as it is produced costs nothing extra. Streaming is not a UI flourish layered on a finished answer — it is a live view of decode. It is also why extended thinking has real cost: thinking tokens are decoded exactly like visible ones, priced as output, and paid for serially before the answer proper begins."}, {"heading": "Memory-bandwidth-bound, not compute-bound", "body": "Each decode step does surprisingly little arithmetic — one token's worth — but must read an enormous amount of memory: essentially all the model's (active) weights, plus the accumulated K/V state for every prior token in the context. The accelerator spends most of each step moving bytes from memory rather than multiplying numbers. Decode speed is therefore set by memory bandwidth, the opposite regime from compute-bound prefill.\n\nTwo practical consequences. First, tokens-per-second is roughly constant for a given model and precision — it is a hardware bandwidth budget divided by bytes that must move per token. Second, anything that shrinks the bytes moved speeds decode up: fewer bits per weight (quantization, 6.6) or fewer active parameters per token (MoE, 6.7) both raise tokens-per-second for exactly this reason."}, {"heading": "Every step reads the whole KV cache", "body": "Decode does not just read weights; each new token attends over the Keys and Values of every token before it — the entire KV cache that prefill built plus everything decoded so far. The cache grows by one entry per layer per generated token, so long contexts make each decode step read more state and consume more accelerator memory per request.\n\nThis is where the phases connect: prefill's output is decode's input. On a cache hit, the stored prefix K/V is loaded rather than recomputed, the new suffix prefills for real, and decode then reads from the whole assembled state exactly as if everything had been computed fresh. Note what the KV cache is not: it is per-request, ephemeral working state on the serving side (persisted briefly by prompt caching under a 5-minute or 1-hour TTL) — not a transcript, and nothing to do with what Claude Code stores in `~/.claude/`."}, {"heading": "Why output costs more and what you can do about it", "body": "The roughly 5× output-to-input price ratio is the two phases expressed in dollars. An input token shares a massively parallel batch; an output token monopolizes a serial pass that reads the whole model. Serving output is intrinsically more expensive per token, on Bedrock and the Anthropic API alike — the ratio travels with the model, whichever door you use.\n\nThe latency split matters for reasoning about sessions: time-to-first-token is prefill, tokens-per-second afterward is decode, and total wall-clock for a verbose reply is dominated by decode. The levers follow directly: constrain output where you can (ask for diffs rather than full files, set `max_tokens` sensibly in scripts), remember thinking is billed as output, and note the asymmetry when budgeting — a Claude Code session's token volume is overwhelmingly input (the resend), but output still claims an outsized share of spend and nearly all of the waiting."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude platform docs — Glossary", "url": "https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/about-claude/glossary"}]}, "6.4": {"title": "Architecture basics", "intro": "You do not need to build a transformer to reason about one. Four concepts — tokens, the layer stack, attention, and the output distribution — are enough to explain where the KV cache comes from, why context has a cost, and why the model never literally 'chooses a word'.", "sections": [{"heading": "Tokens, not words", "body": "The model never sees text. A tokenizer first splits your input into tokens — subword chunks averaging around three-quarters of an English word — and maps each to an integer from a fixed vocabulary. Common words are single tokens; rare words, identifiers, and code fragments split into several. This is why token counts, not character counts, govern everything: context window limits, pricing, and rate limits are all denominated in tokens.\n\nEach token ID is then converted to an embedding — a long vector of numbers representing that token's position in a learned space of meaning. From this point on, everything inside the model is vector arithmetic. The practical intuition to keep: dense prose, code, and JSON tokenize differently, and \"how many tokens is this?\" is a real question with a nonobvious answer, not a word count."}, {"heading": "A stack of identical layers", "body": "A transformer is dozens of near-identical blocks stacked in sequence, each containing two main parts: an attention mechanism and a feed-forward network (FFN). Every token's vector passes through the full stack, and each layer transforms it a little — mixing in information from other tokens (attention) and then processing the result (FFN, where the bulk of the parameters live).\n\nThe useful mental model: early layers capture surface structure, later layers increasingly abstract relationships, and by the top of the stack each position's vector encodes what should come next. Nothing about this is per-user or stateful — the stack is the same frozen weights for every request, applied fresh each call. Depth (number of layers) and width (vector size) are the two main dials that parameter count is made of."}, {"heading": "Attention: which earlier tokens matter", "body": "Attention is the mechanism that lets a token consult its context. For every token, each layer computes three vectors: a Query (what am I looking for?), a Key (what do I offer?), and a Value (what content do I carry?). A token's Query is scored against the Keys of all earlier tokens, and the resulting weights decide how much of each earlier token's Value gets blended into its representation. That is, concretely, how \"the function defined 400 lines ago\" influences the token being processed now.\n\nThe Keys and Values are exactly what the KV cache stores (topic 6.2) — computed once per token per layer, reused by every later token. And because each token attends over all before it, attention work grows with context length: long contexts are expensive not by decree but because there is genuinely more to look at."}, {"heading": "The output is a distribution, not a word", "body": "At the top of the stack, the model produces a score for every token in its vocabulary, normalized into a probability distribution: perhaps 62% for one continuation, 20% for another, a long tail for the rest. The model's job ends there. Something must then pick one token from that distribution — that step is sampling, controlled by parameters like temperature (topic 6.8).\n\nThis separation explains two things people find surprising. First, nondeterminism: identical prompts produce identical distributions, but sampling can draw different tokens from them. Second, the granularity of everything downstream: the model commits to one token, the chosen token is appended, and the whole stack runs again for the next one. Every behavior you observe — hedging, code style, stopping — is ultimately this loop of distribution, pick, append, repeat."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude platform docs — Glossary", "url": "https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/about-claude/glossary"}]}, "6.5": {"title": "Parameters and model size", "intro": "\"How big is the model?\" hides three separate questions — how many weights it has, how much memory it occupies, and how good it is. Keeping them apart is what lets you reason about cost, speed, and model choice instead of assuming bigger is better.", "sections": [{"heading": "What a parameter count means", "body": "A \"200B model\" has roughly 200 billion learned weights — the numbers adjusted during training that collectively encode everything the model can do. Parameter count is capacity: more weights can store more patterns, more knowledge, more nuanced behavior. It is the single most-quoted number about any model, and the least sufficient on its own.\n\nMemory footprint follows from it mechanically: size ≈ parameters (billions) × bits-per-weight ÷ 8, giving gigabytes. A 200B model at 16 bits per weight is about 400 GB of weights — which is why frontier models cannot fit on a single accelerator and are sharded across many GPUs, loaded once and shared read-only by all requests. Parameter count is a fact about the artifact; everything else (quality, speed, price) is downstream but not determined by it."}, {"heading": "The capacity–speed–cost coupling", "body": "Every parameter must be read from memory to produce each output token (with dense models — MoE loosens this, topic 6.7). So parameter count is directly coupled to serving economics: more parameters means more bytes moved per decode step, hence fewer tokens per second, more hardware tied up per request, and a higher price per token. This is why model tiers within a family differ in price by multiples, not percentages.\n\nQuality scales with parameters much less cleanly. Training data quality, training compute, and post-training matter enormously; a newer, well-trained mid-size model frequently outperforms an older giant. Treat parameter count as a strong predictor of cost and latency, and only a rough prior on capability."}, {"heading": "Bigger is not automatically better for a task", "body": "The right question is never \"which model is strongest?\" but \"which model is sufficient for this task?\" Reformatting a file, writing a commit message, or classifying log lines does not benefit from frontier-scale capacity — the small model's answer is indistinguishable, arrives faster, and costs a fraction. Conversely, subtle architectural refactors or long-horizon agentic work can fail outright below a capability threshold, making the cheap model the expensive choice once you count retries.\n\nThis is the reasoning behind per-task model selection in Claude Code (`/model`, or routing subagents to a cheaper tier): match capacity to difficulty, per task, rather than picking one model forever. The Claude family's tier ladder (topic 6.9) exists precisely to make that trade explicit."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude platform docs — Choosing a model", "url": "https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/about-claude/models/choosing-a-model"}]}, "6.6": {"title": "Quantization", "intro": "Quantization stores each of a model's billions of weights with fewer bits — shrinking memory, usually raising speed, and costing some precision. It is the dial serving providers and local runners alike turn to fit models onto real hardware, and the size formula it plugs into is worth memorizing.", "sections": [{"heading": "Fewer bits per weight", "body": "Models are trained with each weight stored at high precision — typically 16 bits, enough to hold a value like `0.7341828`. Quantization rounds every weight onto a coarser grid so it fits in fewer bits: keep `0.73` in 4 bits instead of the full value in 16. The model does not lose parameters or \"get smaller in intelligence\" — the same weights are simply stored more roughly.\n\nThe classic analogy is JPEG compression. A photo at 100% quality is huge and pixel-perfect; at 80% it is a fraction of the size and indistinguishable; pushed too far it grows visible artifacts. Quantization levels are those quality settings, and — as with JPEG — there is a sweet spot well below maximum precision where the loss is essentially invisible, which is why roughly-4-bit schemes became the practical default across the industry."}, {"heading": "The size formula", "body": "Memory footprint is set by exactly two numbers: size (GB) ≈ parameters (billions) × bits-per-weight ÷ 8. The divide-by-8 converts bits to bytes. A 70B model at 16 bits is about 140 GB; the same model at ~4 bits is about 35 GB — a 4× reduction from precision alone, with parameter count untouched.\n\nThis one line lets you reason about any model from its name. It also explains deployment reality at every scale: on a workstation it decides whether a model fits in RAM at all; in a datacenter it decides how many accelerators one replica of a frontier model occupies, and therefore how many concurrent replicas a fleet can run. Remember the weights are not the whole story — the KV cache grows with context on top of them — but weights set the floor."}, {"heading": "The quality–memory–speed triangle", "body": "Quantization trades along three axes at once. Memory: fewer bits, smaller footprint — the direct effect. Speed: often better, and not incidentally — decode is memory-bandwidth-bound (topic 6.3), so halving bytes-per-weight roughly halves the data moved per token, raising tokens-per-second. Quality: each rounded weight is now slightly wrong, and while one tiny error is nothing, errors accumulate across billions of weights and millions of multiplications, surfacing as degraded output — more mistakes, weaker reasoning, subtler failures on hard problems.\n\nThe degradation curve is nonlinear: 16→8 bits is typically near-lossless, ~4 bits is the widely-used sweet spot with modest loss, and below that quality falls off a cliff. Smarter schemes flatten the curve further by spending extra bits on the weights that matter most and economizing on the rest — precision allocated unevenly beats uniform rounding."}, {"heading": "Why providers quantize, and the parameters-vs-precision rule", "body": "Serving providers quantize for economics: a model at lower precision needs fewer accelerators per replica, serves more requests per machine, and decodes faster. Providers choose operating points that preserve quality on their benchmarks — but the mechanism is worth knowing, because \"the same model\" served at different precisions can genuinely behave slightly differently, and precision is one of several serving choices invisible from the API.\n\nThe most useful decision rule the triangle produces: when a memory budget forces a choice, a bigger model at lower precision usually beats a smaller model at higher precision. Parameters store capability; precision only preserves it. Spending bytes on more weights, stored roughly, tends to win over fewer weights stored exactly — the same logic, at datacenter scale, that shapes which capability-per-dollar points a model family's tiers occupy."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude platform docs — Glossary", "url": "https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/about-claude/glossary"}]}, "6.7": {"title": "MoE — mixture of experts", "intro": "Mixture of Experts is the architecture trick that breaks the assumption \"big model = slow model\": a network can hold a huge number of total parameters while only a small fraction do work on any given token. It splits every size-based intuition into two numbers — total and active — and you need both.", "sections": [{"heading": "Many experts, few chosen", "body": "Recall the transformer block: attention plus a feed-forward network (FFN), with the FFN holding most of the parameters. MoE replaces that single FFN with many parallel FFNs — the \"experts\" — plus a tiny router network that, for each token at each layer, picks a small subset (say, the top 2 of 128) to actually run. Every other expert sits idle for that token.\n\nThe result is a model with enormous total capacity — all experts exist and hold learned knowledge — but small per-token compute, since only the chosen few fire. Different tokens route to different experts, so across a batch all the capacity gets exercised, while each individual token travels a cheap path. One caveat for honesty: Anthropic has not published Claude's architecture; MoE is standard frontier-scale design, so treat it as how models of this class generally work, not a confirmed fact about Claude specifically."}, {"heading": "Total vs active parameters", "body": "MoE forces a split in the number you used to treat as one. Total parameters count every weight in every expert — this sets memory (all experts must be loaded and resident; the router's choice happens at inference time, per token, far too fast to swap weights in and out) and sets knowledge capacity. Active parameters count the weights that actually compute for one token — this sets speed and per-token serving cost.\n\nA \"large total, small active\" model therefore takes the memory of a large model but generates at the speed of a small one. For fit-style questions (how many GPUs does a replica need?), use total. For speed and cost questions (tokens per second, price per token), use active. Any single-number description of an MoE model is answering only one of those two questions — always ask which."}, {"heading": "What MoE changes about cost and latency reasoning", "body": "The economics follow from active parameters. Decode is memory-bandwidth-bound, and per token an MoE moves only the active experts' weights — so it decodes at small-model speed and small-model marginal cost despite big-model capacity. This is a core reason frontier providers can serve highly capable models at prices and latencies that dense models of equal capacity could not sustain, and it bends the capability-per-dollar curve that a tiered model family (topic 6.9) is built on.\n\nWhat MoE complicates is the serving side: experts are spread across GPUs (expert parallelism), tokens must shuffle to whichever GPU holds their chosen expert at each layer, and schedulers prefer batches that keep all experts evenly busy. None of that is visible from the API — you see its effects only in the price and speed."}, {"heading": "What MoE does not change", "body": "Everything you learned about the two-phase call survives intact. MoE replaces the FFN, not attention — so the KV cache is built and consumed exactly as before, prefill is still parallel reading and decode still serial writing; the router simply runs per token inside each layer. Routing is deterministic (same tokens, same weights, same expert choices), so prompt caching works unchanged: a cached prefix is just as reusable on an MoE model as on a dense one.\n\nThe stateless-loop story is likewise untouched: weights (all experts included) are loaded once and shared read-only across users, no per-user state survives between calls, and the harness still resends everything each turn. The one-line summary: MoE is a capacity trick on the FFN that leaves attention, the KV cache, and the whole resend-and-cache story exactly as you learned them — it only changes which numbers govern memory versus speed."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude platform docs — Glossary", "url": "https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/about-claude/glossary"}]}, "6.8": {"title": "Sampling and stop conditions", "intro": "The model's forward pass ends with a probability distribution, not a word — something must pick a token from it, and something must decide when to stop picking. Those two mechanisms explain why identical prompts differ between runs and why a response can end for four different reasons.", "sections": [{"heading": "The model proposes, sampling disposes", "body": "Each decode step produces probabilities over the entire vocabulary: one continuation at 62%, another at 20%, a long tail of alternatives. Sampling is the draw from that distribution, and it happens once per token, thousands of times per response — every draw a fork where the output could have gone another way.\n\nThis is the root cause of nondeterminism. Two identical requests produce identical distributions (same tokens, same frozen weights), but independent draws can select different tokens, and the first divergence compounds: once one word differs, every subsequent distribution is conditioned on a different prefix. So \"why did I get a different answer to the same prompt?\" has a precise answer — the model was equally uncertain both times; the dice landed differently. Sampling parameters exist to control how loaded those dice are."}, {"heading": "Temperature and top-p", "body": "Temperature reshapes the distribution before the draw. Low temperature sharpens it — probability concentrates on the top candidates, and at temperature 0 the draw collapses to always taking the single most likely token, making output essentially deterministic (small serving-side numerical effects can still occasionally vary a token). High temperature flattens it, giving unlikely tokens a real chance: more diverse, more creative, more error-prone.\n\nTop-p (nucleus sampling) trims the candidate pool instead: keep only the most probable tokens whose probabilities sum to p, then sample within that nucleus — cutting off the long tail of junk while preserving variety among plausible options. These are request parameters on the Messages API, identical through Bedrock. Practical guidance: adjust temperature or top-p, not both; low temperature suits deterministic-leaning tasks like code and extraction, higher suits brainstorming. Interactive Claude Code sessions use the harness's chosen settings — this is a lever for API scripting, not a per-turn CLI knob."}, {"heading": "Stop conditions and stop reasons", "body": "Decode would happily continue forever, so every response ends because a stop condition fired, and the API tells you which via `stop_reason`. The natural ending is `end_turn`: the model emitted its trained end-of-turn token — it considers the reply complete. `max_tokens` means the hard output ceiling cut generation off mid-thought; the response is truncated, not finished, and treating it as complete is a classic scripting bug. `stop_sequence` means a caller-supplied string appeared and generation halted there — useful for stopping at a delimiter in structured pipelines. And `tool_use` means the model stopped because it is requesting a tool call and expects a result back.\n\nThe first three are model-layer mechanics; the fourth is the hinge the whole agentic loop swings on — a stop that is really a handoff."}, {"heading": "Two stops at two layers", "body": "Keep the layers distinct: a model stop ends one decode; a harness stop ends one turn. When Claude Code's model call returns with `stop_reason: tool_use`, that decode is over — but the turn is not. The harness executes the tool, appends the result to the message array, and calls the API again; this can repeat dozens of times. Only when a call comes back with no tool use (`end_turn`, plain text) does the harness conclude the turn and return control to you.\n\nConflating these causes real confusion: \"the model responded\" is ambiguous between \"one API call completed\" and \"Claude finished the task.\" One user turn commonly spans many decodes, each a separate billed API call with its own stop reason — which is also why a single \"do X\" instruction can quietly cost a long chain of calls."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude platform docs — Glossary", "url": "https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/about-claude/glossary"}, {"label": "Claude API reference — Messages", "url": "https://docs.claude.com/en/api/messages"}]}, "6.9": {"title": "The Claude model family", "intro": "Claude ships as a family of tiers, not a single model — a deliberate capability-cost-speed ladder. The tiers exist so you can match model to task instead of paying frontier prices for routine work; knowing the ladder's shape matters more than any week's specific version numbers.", "sections": [{"heading": "The tier ladder", "body": "The family spans three enduring tiers. Haiku is the fast, inexpensive tier: lowest latency, lowest price per token, built for high-volume and simple tasks. Sonnet is the middle tier: strong capability at moderate cost, the workhorse for most coding and agentic work. Opus is the frontier tier: maximum capability for the hardest reasoning, priced and paced accordingly. Versions within each tier advance over time (and a newer generation's middle tier can overtake an older generation's top tier), but the ladder structure itself is stable.\n\nThe price spread between tiers is multiples — often an order of magnitude between the bottom and the top per token. That spread compounds with the mechanics you already know: every turn resends the whole conversation, so the tier multiplier applies to every token of every resend, not just to new text. Tier choice is the single largest cost lever in the stack."}, {"heading": "Choosing per task, not forever", "body": "The ladder is a mechanism, and the mechanism is per-task matching. Ask what the task needs to not fail: reformatting, summarizing, commit messages, and simple classification are Haiku-shaped; everyday feature work, debugging, and multi-step agentic sessions are Sonnet-shaped; deep architectural reasoning and problems where retries are costly justify Opus. Over-provisioning wastes money on every resent token; under-provisioning wastes more when a failed attempt must be redone on a bigger model anyway.\n\nClaude Code exposes this lever directly: `/model` switches mid-session (or model flags/env vars at launch), and subagents can run on a cheaper tier than the main loop — delegating search-and-summarize work to a fast model while the frontier model keeps the main thread. The skill worth having is not knowing the best model; it is habitually asking \"what is the cheapest tier that reliably does this?\""}, {"heading": "Availability per provider", "body": "The same weights answer through every door — a given Claude model on Bedrock is the same model as on the Anthropic API; Bedrock is a different entrance, not a different brain. What differs is identity and timing. Anthropic uses model names/aliases; Bedrock uses its own model IDs and inference profiles, and availability varies by AWS region — so the identifier in your `/model` output or env config looks different even when the model is identical.\n\nTiming is the practical delta: new model versions and some API features can arrive on Bedrock later than on the Anthropic API, and a new model may reach some regions before others. So a Bedrock user's ladder can briefly lag the newest rung. When choosing per task, the reasoning is provider-neutral; when checking what you can actually invoke today, check your provider's current model list and your region."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude platform docs — Models overview", "url": "https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/about-claude/models/overview"}, {"label": "Claude platform docs — Choosing a model", "url": "https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/about-claude/models/choosing-a-model"}]}, "7.1": {"title": "Setup & auth: env vars vs /login", "intro": "Claude Code reaches the same model through different doors, and each door has its own lock. On the Anthropic API you authenticate to Anthropic; on Bedrock you authenticate to AWS, and Claude Code never sees an Anthropic credential at all.", "sections": [{"heading": "Two doors, two credential systems", "body": "On the Anthropic API you either run `/login` (OAuth against a Claude subscription) or set `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`. Either way, the credential is issued by Anthropic and identifies you to Anthropic's servers.\n\nOn Bedrock there is no Anthropic credential anywhere in the pipeline. Claude Code signs each request with AWS credentials, exactly like any other AWS SDK client calling any other AWS service. That's why `/login` isn't part of the Bedrock workflow and `/logout` is unavailable when Bedrock is active: there's no Anthropic session to log out of. Your identity is your IAM identity, and \"who can use Claude\" becomes an IAM policy question, not an API-key distribution question.\n\nThe switch itself is one environment variable: `CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK=1`. Everything above the API layer — the CLI, the harness, your local files — is untouched by which door you use."}, {"heading": "Region and the setup wizard", "body": "Bedrock is a regional service, so Claude Code must resolve a region for every request. The precedence is: `AWS_REGION`, then `AWS_DEFAULT_REGION`, then the `region` on your active AWS profile, then a `us-east-1` fallback. `/status` shows what was resolved and where it came from.\n\nYou don't have to export variables by hand. The `/setup-bedrock` wizard (also offered at first login under \"3rd-party platform\") detects AWS profiles from `~/.aws`, verifies which Claude models your account can actually invoke, lets you pin them, and writes the result into the `env` block of `~/.claude/settings.json`. This is the same configuration-landing mechanism as any other env-var driven setting: the settings file is just a durable way to put variables into the process environment."}, {"heading": "The AWS credential chain", "body": "Claude Code uses the standard AWS SDK default credential chain: `aws configure` files, environment access keys (`AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID`/`AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`/`AWS_SESSION_TOKEN`), an SSO profile via `AWS_PROFILE` after `aws sso login`, or an Amazon Bedrock API key via `AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK` — the last one being the closest Bedrock gets to \"just paste a key\".\n\nResolved credentials are cached in memory and reused until shortly before expiry, so an SSO profile isn't re-queried on every turn. For corporate identity flows there are two settings hooks: `awsAuthRefresh` runs a command (like `aws sso login`) only when credentials are detected as expired, while `awsCredentialExport` runs at session start and on each reload and must print credentials as JSON. Use the first when the command can update `~/.aws`; the second only when it can't."}, {"heading": "IAM permissions and one-time model access", "body": "Two AWS-side prerequisites exist that have no Anthropic-side equivalent. First, a one-time use case form per AWS account: before an account can invoke Anthropic models, someone submits use case details in the Bedrock console (or via the `PutUseCaseForModelAccess` API once for a whole AWS Organization). Access is granted immediately after submission.\n\nSecond, the calling role needs IAM permissions: `bedrock:InvokeModel` and `bedrock:InvokeModelWithResponseStream` on the relevant foundation-model and inference-profile ARNs, plus `bedrock:ListInferenceProfiles` and `bedrock:GetInferenceProfile` so Claude Code can discover and resolve profiles. Administrators can scope the Resource list to specific profile ARNs — which is exactly the governance lever enterprises want: model access controlled by the same policy machinery as S3 buckets."}, {"heading": "Naming the model", "body": "On the Anthropic API you name models like `claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929`. On Bedrock the same snapshot is addressed as a Bedrock model ID, and in practice as a cross-region inference profile ID with a geography prefix: `us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929-v1:0` (or `eu.`, `apac.`, `us-gov.`).\n\nClaude Code's aliases (`sonnet`, `opus`, `haiku`) resolve to built-in Bedrock defaults, which team deployments should override by pinning: `ANTHROPIC_MODEL` for the session model, and `ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_OPUS_MODEL` / `ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_SONNET_MODEL` / `ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_HAIKU_MODEL` to control what each alias means. The values can also be application inference profile ARNs, which lets an organization route every request through a profile it administers."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude Code docs — Claude Code on Amazon Bedrock", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/amazon-bedrock"}, {"label": "AWS docs — Identity and access management for Amazon Bedrock", "url": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/security-iam.html"}]}, "7.2": {"title": "Feature parity & lag", "intro": "The reliable rule: anything implemented in the harness on your machine is identical on Bedrock and the Anthropic API; anything implemented server-side can differ or arrive later. Sorting a feature into the right bucket predicts whether switching providers changes it.", "sections": [{"heading": "The parity rule: client-side features can't differ", "body": "Recall the four layers: CLI → harness → API → model. The provider swap happens entirely at the API layer — a different endpoint URL, a different signature on the request. Everything the harness does happens before the request leaves your machine or after the response arrives: prompt assembly, `CLAUDE.md` injection, compaction, permissions, hooks, skills, slash commands, subagents, MCP servers, the transcript `*.jsonl`.\n\nNone of that code knows or cares which door the request goes through. Plan mode works the same. Hooks fire the same. A skill triggers the same way. The stateless loop — rebuild the whole conversation, resend it every turn — is identical because the loop lives in the harness, not the server. If a quiz question asks whether some CLI or harness behavior \"works on Bedrock\", the answer is almost always yes, unchanged."}, {"heading": "What is genuinely API-dependent", "body": "Server-side features are where deltas live. The clearest example: the WebSearch tool is not available on Amazon Bedrock — search is executed on Anthropic's servers as a server-side tool, and Bedrock doesn't provide that service, so the harness simply doesn't offer the tool there.\n\nOther API-dependent surfaces: prompt-caching TTL options (which TTLs exist depends on the model and, on Bedrock, on the region), the 1M-token context window (supported for specific models on Bedrock, selected by appending `[1m]` to the model ID), and beta features gated by API headers, which typically appear on the Anthropic API first. Bedrock also adds features Anthropic direct doesn't have: Guardrails content filtering via request headers, and service tiers (`default`, `flex`, `priority`) set with `ANTHROPIC_BEDROCK_SERVICE_TIER`."}, {"heading": "Model lag and alias drift", "body": "New Claude models generally reach the Anthropic API first and Bedrock on AWS's schedule, region by region. Claude Code copes with this in two ways.\n\nFirst, aliases: on Bedrock, `sonnet` and `opus` resolve to built-in defaults compiled into the CLI, which can lag the newest release — and may not even be enabled in your account. Second, startup model checks: when Bedrock is configured, Claude Code verifies its intended models are invokable; if the default isn't available it falls back for the session to an earlier version, or from Opus down to Sonnet, and shows a notice. The fallback is not persisted.\n\nThis is why the docs push pinning for team rollouts: explicit `ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_*_MODEL` values mean users move to a new model when the administrator says so, not when a CLI update changes a default."}, {"heading": "Endpoint shape: Invoke, not Converse — and Mantle", "body": "Bedrock offers two generic inference APIs: Converse (an AWS-normalized schema across all vendors' models) and InvokeModel (a passthrough that carries each vendor's native request body). Claude Code uses the InvokeModel streaming API and does not support Converse — it wants to speak the Anthropic Messages shape it already speaks, merely wrapped in a Bedrock envelope.\n\nAWS also operates the Mantle endpoint (`CLAUDE_CODE_USE_MANTLE=1`), which serves Claude through the native Anthropic API shape rather than the Invoke wrapper, with its own model lineup (IDs like `anthropic.claude-haiku-4-5`, no version suffix) and separate quotas. Both can be enabled at once; Claude Code routes by model ID format. The trend is a parity story: the more the AWS door speaks native Anthropic protocol, the fewer feature deltas survive."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude Code docs — Claude Code on Amazon Bedrock", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/amazon-bedrock"}, {"label": "Claude Code docs — Model configuration", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/model-config"}]}, "7.3": {"title": "Caching differences: Bedrock vs Anthropic", "intro": "Prompt caching is the single biggest cost lever for an agent that resends its entire conversation every turn — and it works on both providers with the same mechanism. The deltas are in TTL options, per-model minimums, and regional availability, not in how the cache works.", "sections": [{"heading": "Same mechanism through either door", "body": "On both providers, caching means: the server stores the computed attention state (KV cache) for a prompt prefix, keyed by exact prefix match, and reuses it when the next request starts with byte-identical content. Claude Code marks breakpoints with `cache_control: {\"type\": \"ephemeral\"}` blocks in the request body — and because Claude Code talks to Bedrock through InvokeModel with the native Anthropic body, it sets breakpoints identically on both providers. (Bedrock's Converse API spells the same thing `cachePoint`, but Claude Code doesn't use Converse.)\n\nThe invalidation rules are also shared: prefixes chain in the order `tools` → `system` → `messages`, up to 4 breakpoints per request, and any edit to an earlier section invalidates everything after it. This is exactly why the harness maintains its stability gradient — stable prefix first, volatile content last."}, {"heading": "The pricing structure", "body": "Both providers price caching with the same three-way split: cache writes cost more than base input, cache reads cost far less, and unmatched tokens bill at the normal input rate.\n\nOn the Anthropic API the documented multipliers are: 5-minute-TTL cache writes at 1.25× base input, 1-hour-TTL writes at 2× base input, and cache reads at 0.1× base input. Bedrock's documentation describes the same structure — reads at a reduced rate, writes potentially above the uncached rate — with exact numbers on the Amazon Bedrock pricing page per model.\n\nOne shared subtlety: a cache hit refreshes the TTL at no additional cost. A Claude Code session that keeps sending turns inside the TTL window keeps its cache alive indefinitely, paying the write once and reads thereafter. Walk away past the TTL and the next turn pays a fresh write of the whole prefix."}, {"heading": "TTL options and the Claude Code knobs", "body": "The default TTL is 5 minutes on both providers, refreshed on every hit. The 1-hour TTL is where support diverges: on Bedrock it's available only on specific models — AWS's table lists 5-minute and 1-hour support per model ID (for example, Sonnet 4.5 and Haiku 4.5 list both; some models list only 5 minutes). Requesting it means adding `\"ttl\": \"1h\"` to the `cache_control` object.\n\nClaude Code exposes this as environment variables: `ENABLE_PROMPT_CACHING_1H=1` requests the 1-hour TTL (billed at the higher write rate — worth it when your gaps between turns exceed 5 minutes but not an hour), and `DISABLE_PROMPT_CACHING=1` turns caching off entirely, which is mainly a compatibility escape hatch for gateways or regions where caching misbehaves. When both TTLs appear in one request, longer-TTL entries must precede shorter ones."}, {"heading": "Bedrock-specific constraints", "body": "A few constraints exist only on the AWS side. Minimum cacheable prefix length varies by model — 1,024 tokens for some Claude models, 4,096 for others; below the minimum the request still succeeds but nothing is cached, silently. Caching is supported only for on-demand inference, not batch. And it isn't available in every region: the diagnostic is cache token counts stuck at zero in `/context` or usage output, and the fix is checking AWS's supported-models-and-regions table.\n\nCross-region inference adds a wrinkle: because your requests can be routed to different regions at times of high demand, and the KV cache lives where the request was served, routing changes can produce extra cache writes. That's a cost blip, not a correctness problem — a missed cache is always just recomputed.\n\nBedrock also reports usage differently: `inputTokens` counts only non-cached tokens, so total input = `inputTokens` + `cacheReadInputTokens` + `cacheWriteInputTokens`."}], "resources": [{"label": "AWS docs — Prompt caching for faster model inference", "url": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/prompt-caching.html"}, {"label": "Claude Platform docs — Prompt caching", "url": "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching"}]}, "7.4": {"title": "Throughput & limits", "intro": "Both providers meter you, but with different machinery: Anthropic applies per-organization rate limit tiers, while Bedrock applies AWS Service Quotas per model, per region, per account. The failure modes and the escape hatches differ accordingly.", "sections": [{"heading": "Two different governors", "body": "On the Anthropic API, limits are organization-level rate limits — requests per minute and input/output tokens per minute — that scale with your usage tier. Exceeding one returns an HTTP 429 with a retry-after header, and the SDK-style response tells you exactly which limit you hit.\n\nOn Bedrock, model inference is controlled by AWS Service Quotas: each model has its own requests-per-minute and tokens-per-minute quotas, tracked per region and per account, viewable in the Service Quotas console. Exceeding them produces a `ThrottlingException`. Many quotas are adjustable by request; defaults also vary by account history and region. Two consequences follow from the per-model-per-region scoping: your Sonnet quota and Opus quota are independent pools, and moving regions means a different quota allocation. Bedrock's two endpoints (`bedrock-runtime` and `bedrock-mantle`) even track separate quotas for the same model."}, {"heading": "Why a coding agent is hard on token quotas", "body": "Remember the keystone: the harness resends the entire conversation every turn. A long Claude Code session therefore consumes input tokens superlinearly — turn 50 resends everything from turns 1–49 — and tokens-per-minute is usually the quota you hit first, not requests-per-minute.\n\nCaching is the counterweight, and it interacts with limits directly: per AWS's guidance for Claude models, cache hits are not deducted against your rate limit, so a well-cached session burns quota mostly for the new tail of each turn rather than the whole prefix.\n\nAgent fan-out multiplies all of this. Every subagent runs its own loop with its own resend, in parallel, against the same per-model per-region quota pool. A team of five agents can throttle an account that a single interactive session never troubled — which is why quota planning is part of multi-agent design on Bedrock."}, {"heading": "Cross-region inference profiles", "body": "The `us.` in `us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929-v1:0` is not decoration — it names a cross-region inference profile, Bedrock's mechanism for absorbing bursts. A geographic profile defines a model plus a set of regions within one geography (US, EU, APAC), and Bedrock routes each request to the optimal region in that set; a global profile can route to any supported commercial region for the highest throughput, with roughly 10% cost savings.\n\nKey properties: there's no extra routing charge (you pay the price of your calling region), traffic stays on the AWS network encrypted in transit, destination regions don't need to be manually enabled, and CloudTrail records where each request actually ran (`additionalEventData.inferenceRegion`). Geographic profiles exist precisely for data-residency rules; global for maximum headroom. For newer Claude models, inference profiles are effectively mandatory — invoking the bare foundation-model ID returns an \"on-demand throughput isn't supported\" error."}, {"heading": "Buying guaranteed capacity", "body": "When on-demand quotas aren't enough or predictability matters more than elasticity, Bedrock sells Provisioned Throughput: you purchase Model Units, each delivering a defined input and output tokens-per-minute rate, billed hourly, with commitment options of none, 1 month, or 6 months (longer commitments, lower hourly price). You invoke provisioned capacity by using its ARN as the model ID. Note that inference profiles don't support Provisioned Throughput — it's a separate path.\n\nA lighter-weight lever is service tiers: `ANTHROPIC_BEDROCK_SERVICE_TIER` set to `default`, `flex`, or `priority` trades cost against latency per request via a header, no capacity purchase involved.\n\nThe Anthropic-direct analogue to all of this is simpler but less granular: tiers raise automatically with usage, and beyond that you talk to sales — there's no self-service reserved-capacity purchase or region arithmetic."}, {"heading": "Failure modes and retries", "body": "The practical differences show up when things go wrong. Anthropic-direct overload is a 429 (or 529 when the API itself is overloaded) — back off and retry. Bedrock throttling is a `ThrottlingException`, and there's a second, distinct failure family: regional capacity and availability errors, where a model simply isn't available or is at capacity in the region you targeted. Cross-region profiles are the standing mitigation for the latter; quota increase requests for the former.\n\nClaude Code retries transient errors itself, so as a user you mostly see slowdowns, not crashes. But diagnosis differs by door: on Bedrock, check the Service Quotas console and CloudWatch metrics, confirm you're calling an inference profile rather than a bare model ID, and remember quotas are per model per region — switching model or region switches pools."}], "resources": [{"label": "AWS docs — Increase throughput with cross-Region inference", "url": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/cross-region-inference.html"}, {"label": "AWS docs — Quotas for Amazon Bedrock", "url": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/quotas.html"}, {"label": "AWS docs — Provisioned Throughput in Amazon Bedrock", "url": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/prov-throughput.html"}]}, "7.5": {"title": "Billing & governance", "intro": "Token prices aside, the two doors bill and govern completely differently: Anthropic direct is a vendor relationship with its own console and keys; Bedrock folds Claude into the AWS machinery your organization already runs — billing, IAM, audit, and network controls included.", "sections": [{"heading": "Where the money flows", "body": "Anthropic direct offers two payment shapes: subscription plans (Pro/Max), where `/login` attaches Claude Code usage to a flat monthly fee with usage limits, and API pay-per-token, metered per API key and workspace in the Claude Console.\n\nOn Bedrock there is no subscription option — it's pay-per-token on your AWS bill, priced per model on the Amazon Bedrock pricing page, landing in the same invoice as EC2 and S3. That single fact is most of the enterprise pitch: no new vendor onboarding, no separate procurement, spend visible in Cost Explorer alongside everything else, existing AWS discount agreements potentially applying.\n\nThe docs' recommendation of a dedicated AWS account for Claude Code is a billing tactic: account boundaries are the coarsest, most reliable cost-attribution line AWS has, making \"what does Claude Code cost us\" a one-filter Cost Explorer query."}, {"heading": "Cost attribution machinery", "body": "Below the account level, Bedrock's finer-grained attribution tool is the application inference profile: a profile ARN you create that wraps a model, can carry cost-allocation tags, and is invoked as the model ID. Give each team its own profile and per-team spend falls out of standard AWS cost reporting.\n\nClaude Code cooperates with this via configuration: `ANTHROPIC_MODEL` accepts an application inference profile ARN directly, and the `modelOverrides` setting maps model versions in the `/model` picker to specific ARNs — so users switch models freely while every request still flows through a profile the organization administers and meters.\n\nAnthropic direct's equivalent is workspaces and per-key reporting in the Claude Console — adequate for API-key accounting, but it's a separate reporting system rather than lines in the bill your finance team already processes."}, {"heading": "Access control and audit", "body": "On Bedrock, who may use which model is an IAM question: `bedrock:InvokeModel` permissions scoped to specific model or inference-profile ARNs, enforceable org-wide with Service Control Policies (cross-region profiles interact with SCPs — geographic profiles need their destination regions allowed). Access reviews, credential rotation, and least-privilege tooling that already govern the rest of AWS govern Claude too.\n\nEvery invocation is logged in CloudTrail — including, for cross-region inference, which region actually served the request. Bedrock Guardrails add centrally administered content filtering: Claude Code attaches a guardrail ID and version as request headers via `ANTHROPIC_CUSTOM_HEADERS`, so policy is enforced server-side where users can't switch it off.\n\nAnthropic direct governance operates through Console roles, workspace scoping, and key management — real controls, but a parallel system rather than an extension of existing cloud governance."}, {"heading": "Why enterprises pick Bedrock", "body": "The standard reasons compose from the above plus network and residency posture. Traffic can stay on private network paths via VPC interface endpoints (AWS PrivateLink) instead of the public internet. Data residency is addressable: geographic cross-region inference profiles guarantee processing stays within a chosen geography (US, EU, APAC), and even cross-region traffic remains on the encrypted AWS backbone. Bedrock inherits AWS's compliance certifications, so Claude often clears security review as \"another AWS service\" rather than a net-new vendor. Neither provider trains on your API traffic; the difference is whose paperwork asserts it.\n\nThe honest trade-off runs the other way in velocity: Anthropic direct gets new models and API features first, offers subscription pricing, and requires no IAM or quota engineering. Bedrock buys governance and procurement fit at the cost of some lag and more AWS-side operations work."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude Code docs — Claude Code on Amazon Bedrock", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/amazon-bedrock"}, {"label": "AWS docs — Data protection in Amazon Bedrock", "url": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/data-protection.html"}]}, "7.6": {"title": "What never changes", "intro": "After five lessons of deltas, the anti-confusion lesson: Bedrock is not a different Claude. Same weights, same harness, same protocol — Bedrock is a different door into the same model, and knowing what can't differ is what lets you reason about the rest.", "sections": [{"heading": "Same weights", "body": "There is no \"Bedrock version\" of Claude. Anthropic trains a model, freezes it as a dated snapshot, and that same artifact is what both doors serve. You can read this off the identifiers: `claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929` on the Anthropic API and `us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929-v1:0` on Bedrock share the same date stamp because they name the same snapshot. Bedrock's ID adds routing metadata — the `us.` geography prefix, the `-v1:0` versioning — around it, not a different model inside it.\n\nSo everything in the model branch of this quiz is provider-neutral by construction: the context window, prefill and decode behavior, tokenization, sampling, capability tier. If a colleague reports that \"Claude on Bedrock is dumber,\" the explanation lives in configuration — a lagging default model, a different pinned version, a missing feature like WebSearch — never in the weights."}, {"heading": "Same harness", "body": "The provider switch is one environment variable precisely because it only swaps the API layer. Every mechanism the harness implements runs on your machine, before the request leaves or after the response returns: the stateless loop and the full resend each turn, prompt assembly and the stability gradient, `CLAUDE.md` and `settings.json` resolution, compaction, permissions, hooks, skills, custom commands, subagents, MCP, the transcript `*.jsonl`.\n\nBedrock cannot see any of this, let alone change it. A hook fires identically; a skill triggers identically; `/compact` summarizes identically. The few CLI-level differences that do exist — no `/login` or `/logout`, `/setup-bedrock` appearing in the menu — are auth plumbing at the boundary, not harness behavior. Your muscle memory, your config files, and your mental model of the loop transfer wholesale between providers."}, {"heading": "Same protocol", "body": "Claude Code speaks the Anthropic Messages protocol through both doors. On Bedrock, the same request body — system prompt, messages array, tool schemas, `cache_control` breakpoints — is carried inside Bedrock's InvokeModel API with an `anthropic_version` field marking the dialect. The model emits the same content blocks either way: text, `tool_use` out, `tool_result` back in, the same stop reasons, the same usage accounting concepts.\n\nThe envelope differs — Bedrock streams responses in AWS's binary event-stream format where the Anthropic API uses server-sent events, and AWS SigV4 signatures replace the `x-api-key` header — but the events inside the envelope are the same protocol. This is why tool use, agentic behavior, and prompt caching all work identically: they're properties of the conversation the model sees, and the model sees the same conversation."}, {"heading": "The classification test", "body": "This yields a reliable test for any Bedrock-vs-Anthropic question: ask which layer owns the behavior.\n\nModel layer (weights, context window, reasoning, tokens)? Identical — same snapshot. Harness layer (loop, assembly, compaction, hooks, skills, permissions, files)? Identical — runs locally. API layer? Now check the delta list: authentication (AWS credentials vs API key or OAuth), model naming (inference profile IDs vs Anthropic names), limits (Service Quotas per model per region vs organization rate-limit tiers), billing (AWS bill vs Anthropic Console or subscription), availability timing (new models and betas usually land Anthropic-first), and a handful of server-side features (WebSearch absent on Bedrock; Guardrails and service tiers absent on Anthropic direct).\n\nEverything in branches 1–4 and 6 of this quiz sits in the first two buckets. Only the API layer has two answers — and that entire delta list is about the door, never about the model behind it."}], "resources": [{"label": "Claude Code docs — Claude Code on Amazon Bedrock", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/amazon-bedrock"}, {"label": "Claude Platform docs — Models overview", "url": "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/models/overview"}]}};
